f.d:
{Question: What does this mean?
Reply: This is understood through reasoning that refutes extreme positions, since all objects of knowledge must be either conditioned or unconditioned, conditioned phenomena (saṃskṛta) must be either uncontaminated or contaminated, and unconditioned phenomena (asaṃskṛta) and uncontaminated conditioned phenomena do not act to blind us but act as antidotes [to ignorance]. Therefore they are not darkness since they neither act as darkness nor possess the characteristics of darkness.
Contaminated phenomena have two types: (1) afflicted conditioned phenomena are darkness through and through, and (2) unafflicted conditioned phenomena are contaminated virtue and unobscured-neutral phenomena (akliṣṭāvyākṛta) specifically derived from, say, the four causal states of unknowing.[120]}

Darkness is destroyed by means of buddhas completely destroying and abandoning all knowledge obscurations and their imprints, never to rearise since they attain transcendent wisdom as the antidote to darkness.} “Completely destroyed” (sarvahata) refers to states that due to finding their antidote never rearise. This demonstrates a special abandonment distinct from that of śrāvakas and so on, because although śrāvakas possess the mere abandonment of the obscuration of afflicted {darkness}, it is possible their realizations degenerate {since they do not abandon imprints}.[129] Moreover they do not abandon unafflicted knowledge obscurations in every aspect. {Therefore the Autocommentary declares:
Because pratyekabuddhas and śrāvakas are free from afflicted confusion, they are said to destroy the darkness enveloping all objects. They do not do so completely because they have unafflicted unknowing of a buddha’s qualities, of objects and times that are extremely remote, and of the limitless classifications of objects.[130]
These have been explained.}

Reply: The Master teaches according to the purpose (yathārtha) of others, since he teaches just that path of liberation which they require. {Since the Master possesses altruism, he raises his hand to teach the pure Dharma with the enlightened activity of speech, correctly teaching the meaning of the four noble truths just as they are, and leading disciples from saṃsāra according to their capacity, for this is not achieved by any other means.
Objection: Since saṃsāra and reincarnating beings are not distinct states, it is untenable that saṃsāra is a source term for reincarnating beings.[136]
Reply: There is no error since he leads specific fortunate reincarnating beings from the general class of saṃsāric beings.}

Question: Now why is Abhidharma stated for that need?
Reply: Without teaching Abhidharma in this way, wisdom fully differentiating phenomena would not occur in the mindstreams of disciples.
{Question: What fault is there if you do not have wisdom? What benefit is there if you have wisdom?}

Conditioned phenomena (saṃskṛta), except for the truth of path (mārga), are the truths of origin and suffering, which are contaminated (sāsravā). Why? Because for them contaminants (āsravā) always increase (anuśerate) due to focusing on them or being concomitant[187] with them. Here “contaminants” refers to the root and branch afflictions.[188] “Increase” refers to continuously expanding the field {of attachment or affliction through craving things and viewing them as “mine”}. Or it is because contaminants benefit and assist such {conditions} or because such {conditions} benefit and assist the contaminants.
The Compendium of Abhidharma mentions that something is contaminated due to being related to the six doors of the contaminants:[189] (1) Contaminant itself refers to the root and branch afflictions, which are the essential nature of the contaminants. (2) Related to the contaminants refers to minds and mental factors that are coemergent with the contaminants, or it refers to the dominant condition, such as the eyes, that supports such concomitant states. (3) Bound by the contaminants refers to the contaminated virtue of the three realms, which fully binds to the causes of existence. (4) Subsequently related to the contaminants refers to that which possesses the dysfunction [or error] of the contaminants. (5) Subsequently compatible with the contaminants refers to that which accommodates the contaminants through not being their antidote, or the objects of root and branch afflictions, such as material form, that are focal objects of the contaminants since they increase the contaminants when observed. (6) Derived from the contaminants that are its cause refers to that which is contaminated through being the result of the contaminants.
It is impossible for them to merge as a single contaminated phenomenon because it is impossible for them to converge in a single state. Therefore a contaminated state should be understood to be related in some way to any of these six, since it is meaningless to speak of each separately having these six types. {If something must coexist with the contaminants in order to be contaminated, then only the five appropriated aggregates would be contaminated. But then it would follow that contaminated aggregates associated with a virtuous mind and external contaminated [material form] would be impossible.}

{Its etymology is “that which accommodates.”}
Objection: If space exists at the site where obstructive resistance exists, say in a wall, [21] even space would be physical and obstructed. If not, space would be intermittent and impermanent.
Reply: Since space, which accommodates, possesses a nonphysical nature, it occupies the spatial location of a wall and so on. If it didn’t exist there, the wall and so forth would not exist there because space, which accommodates, would not exist there — and the wall would be blocked by other types of material form. Therefore, though space exists there, it doesn’t become form because its nature is nonphysical — just as, though a body has mind, the mind does not become form.

{The obtainment of both cessations is not simultaneous. To demonstrate} Master Jinaputra explains that there are four possibilities:
(1) Analytical cessation is obtained first, and the other is obtained later. {When arhats resolute in faith abandon objects to be abandoned by the path of meditation, obtainment of analytical cessation occurs. But since it is possible that this state may degenerate, at that time they also upgrade their intellectual faculty and obtain nonanalytical cessation.} [Another] example is the consciousness of an arhat holding one of the five sense objects, such as form, when his or her eye or mind is distracted to another form. {Since the five sense consciousnesses are contaminated, arhats first obtain the analytical cessation of all contaminated phenomena.}
(2) Nonanalytical cessation is obtained first, and the other is obtained later. For example, those who obtain the patience stage of the path of preparation {prevent [the arising of] the aggregates of} a lower-realm being {through nonanalytical cessation and obtain the analytical cessation of those aggregates when the path of meditation arises.}
(3) Both are obtained simultaneously. For example, {those who abandon objects to be abandoned by the path of seeing by obtaining the path of seeing, and} those who obtain their result through abandoning the great-of-the-great object to be abandoned by the path of meditation {obtain the nonanalytical cessation of the great stage not yet arisen, and those beings resolute in faith who reside in the desire realm or above, obtain them simultaneously.

{'di dus dang mi sbyar bar grol ba la ci ste mi mtshungs zhe na/ de ni mi slob pa yin pas 'thob byed ma yin no zhes 'chad do}/ gnyis ka mi thob pa ni de las gzhan pa lta bu'o zhes bya ba'i mu bzhi yang bshad do/{ de lta bu la sogs pa mu bzhi brtsi ba'i gzhi ni 'gal 'brel med pa ste/ dngos 'gal zag bcas zag med lta bu la/ phung gsum sel bas mu tha ma gnyis med cing*/ brgyud 'gal sngo ser lta bu la ngo bo 'gal bas mu gsum pa med la/ bdag gcig pa'i 'brel pa byas mi rtag lta bu la mu dang po gnyis med cing*/ de las byung ba'i 'brel pa me du lta bu la mu gsum pa med pa'i phyir ro/ /
mu bzhi rtsis pa'i dgos pa ni shes bya la mkhas par 'gyur ba'o/ /
sgra don ni mu zhes pa mtha' ste/ dri ba de lta bu la ltos nas lan mtha' bzhi kho nar nges kyi lnga pa la sogs pa med pa'i don to/ /}

{ kha cig rnam par rig byed ma yin pa'i gzugs nags la me zhugs pa ltar lus thams cad la khyab par gnas par bye brag tu smra ba 'dod do zhes 'chad pa ni mi rigs te/ gzugs med khams na gnas med do/ /
zhes pa'i 'grel pa dang TIkar rnam par rig byed ma yin pa ni gzugs can yin la da ltar gyi dag kyang yul na mi gnas zhes bya bar nges pa yin no zhes pa dang gnas bzhi par rnam rig byed min ma zin dang zhes dbang po'i tshogs pa na med pas sems dang sems byung gi rten du mi rung ba dang*/ rdul phran bsags pa'i gzugs ma yin pas gzugs med ltar yul gang na'ang mi gnas par bshad pa'i phyir ro/ /
des na 'di ni lus kyi rig byed la la rig byed ma yin par 'khrul ba kho nar zad do/}

'grel pas gzugs dang byed pa'i rang bzhin yin yang rnam par rig byed bzhin du gzhan dag la rnam par rig par byed pa ma yin pas rnam par rig byed ma yin pa'o/ /
zhes 'byung bas rdul phran bsags pa'i gzugs ma yin yang 'byung bzhi rgyur byas pa'i gzugs dang byed pa lus ngag gi las kyi rang bzhin yin yang lus ngag gi rnam par rig byed bzhin du rang kun nas slong ba'i bsam pa dge mi dge la sogs pa gzhan dag la rnam par rig cing go bar mi byed pas na rnam par rig byed ma yin pa zhes bya ste/ 'di ni dgag pa gnyis las ma yin par dgag pa dang de las kyang rgyal po la sogs pa bram ze dang mi 'dra ba la bram ze ma yin pa zhes zer ba ltar rnam par rig byed dang mi 'dra bas 'dra ba la dgag pa yin no zhes slob dpon gang spel 'chad do/ /
de nyid kyi phyir slob dpon rgyal po'i sras kyis kyang dngos po gang zhig gzugs yin la byed pa dang gzhan la go byed ma yin pa mig sogs dbang po lnga dang dri ro reg bya dang lus ngag gi rig byed ma yin pa'i gzugs sgra lta bu dang*/ byed pa las kyi rang bzhin yin la gzugs dang gzhan la go bar mi byed pa yid kyi las sems pa lta bu dang gzugs dang byed pa dang*/ gzhan la go byed gsum ga yin pa lus ngag gi rnam par rig byed lta bus gzugs dang byed pa yin la/ gzhan la go bar mi byed pa rnam par rig byed ma yin pa lta bu'o zhes pa'i mu bzhi bshad do/}

Master Saṅghabhadra writes:
(1) It does not fulfill [the definition], (2) it contradicts the treatise, (3) it would fail, and not being so it is erroneous, (4) the term “also” is repeated, and (5) it does not delineate action.[271]
He asserts: (1) If nonindicative form must be continuously “related” (1:11c), then because the first moment of nonindicative form is not a continuum, it would not fulfill the definition. (2) If it were not substantially established because its continuum were not substantially existent, it would contradict the treatise,[272] which explains it to be physical substance. (3) If it must be related to “a distracted mind and also mindless states” (1:11a), then {since there would be no} nonindicative form derived from concentration, the definition would fail, since it could not be associated with undistracted minds. But if it is asserted that there is no error since nonindicative form is related to the undistracted mind and {exists} when the mind is present, then it would follow that even {virtuous or nonvirtuous} indicative form would be nonindicative form. But since that is not so, that assertion is erroneous. (4) The term “also” is repeated {in the Autocommentary} because a relationship is demonstrated when the mind is distracted and a relationship is understood when the mind is present. (5) It does not delineate specific action because it does not demarcate undemonstrable from unimpeded form. {For existing in mindless states necessarily and specifically indicates virtuous nonindicative form, but if nonvirtue is not abandoned, then neither meditative attainment occurs.}

{If classified by focal object, the Compendium of Abhidharma explains six. (1) Discernment engaging marks is all discernment except for the three: (a) discernment of children who for example do not know abstract symbols and who are not skilled in linguistic conventions; (b) discernment of those entering meditative attainment on the signless sphere of cessation; and (c) discernment of those entering meditative attainment of the summit of existence, since discernment engaging marks respectively possesses the marks of superimposed names, the marks of conditioned objects, and clear marks. (2) Discernment not engaging marks is the three discernments, such as discernment of those not skilled in linguistic conventions and so on [as in the preceding discernment], since, respectively, (a) it does not engage the marks of superimposed names through not knowing how to utilize the fusion of sound universals and object universals; (b) it does not engage either the marks of superimposed names or conditioned objects since its object, reality itself (dharmatā) clearly appears to it; and (c) it does not engage clear marks, since the summit of existence is not clear due to its natural fluctuation. (3) Limited discernment is discernment of the desire realm. (4) Extensive discernment is discernment of the form realm. (5) Immeasurable discernment is discernment of the first two formless-realm levels. (6) Discernment of nothingness is knowledge of the base of nothingness.[285]}
Objection: If discernment exists for the transcendent wisdom of āryas in equipoise, they would apprehend marks, and this would contradict the explanation that they are liberated from apprehending marks. But if they have no discernment, then discernment would not be an omnipresent mental factor.
Reply: Indeed, even ārya transcendent wisdom apprehends the difference between pleasure and pain or clarity and unclarity. The explanation that they are liberated from marks means they are liberated from conceiving the two selves. Therefore neither error applies.

{Objection: Since the six consciousnesses and the mental faculty mutually include each other, if the mental faculty is primary there would be twelve elements, but if consciousness is primary there would be seventeen elements.
Reply: Though the actual presentation of the elements conforms with the latter presentation, there is no fault because this presentation depends on the need [of students]. Strictly speaking, present minds and mental factors are not the mental faculty since the mental faculty “immediately precedes” (1:17ab). And though the present mental faculty is stably established, at different times it is [designated] an element, just as a son may also be designated the father of another. The five consciousnesses are distinct elements since they generate subsequent minds of similar type [to themselves]. After the cessation of their specific streams, they are posited as the mental element generated by the mental faculty that engages their specific objects.
Internal elements are distinct in three ways: type, object field, and consciousness. External elements are distinct in type and consciousness. The consciousness elements are distinct in type and object field. The phenomena element (dharmadhātu) is the specific object field of the mental faculty, and the six consciousnesses constitute the mental element in general. That which immediately precedes [consciousness] and is not obstructed by another consciousness is its support. That which obstructs is not its support for that which is past, which [endures] until a future time and does not obstruct, is the support. Therefore, in the context of mindless states, if the mind entering meditative attainment is past, it is the support of the mind arising from meditative attainment because it is not obstructed by another consciousness for a long period.}

Reply: Unconditioned phenomena (asaṃskṛta) are not explained (na ukta) among the aggregates because they have an incongruent nature (arthā ayoga). Unconditioned phenomena do not possess the characteristics of the five aggregates; nor could they be a sixth aggregate, because the nature of past phenomena and so on is incongruent with “a heap of many phenomena.” Further, the appropriated aggregates were taught in order to make known [54] the basis of affliction, and the aggregates were taught to make known the basis of both unenlightened and enlightened states, but unconditioned phenomena have a nature that is incongruent with either basis.
Some explain that unconditioned phenomena cannot be aggregates since they preclude the aggregates, just as a broken vase is not a vase.
But that is not sound reasoning, since it would entail an extreme fallacy if it were similarly applied to elements and bases. Also it is due to the focal object that the meaning of “base” is explained as the gateway of generation and that the meaning of “element” is explained as lineage. {The definite number of five aggregates has already been explained.}

{Question: With reference to the statement “should be included” (1:27a), it may be asked: what are included, where are they included, and how are they included?
Reply: It is said, respectively: “aggregates, bases, and elements” (1:27b), “according to the way they were explained” (1:27c), and “having well analyzed their own characteristics” (1:27d). Thus those named aggregates, bases, and elements that occur in other sūtras should respectively be included in the aggregates, bases, and elements as previously explained, for their characteristics have been well analyzed in various ways, and they possess those characteristics.}
Just as the aggregates of Dharma were included within the aggregates, so too others that are designated “aggregates” and so on in other sūtras should be included (pratipādya) within the aggregates, bases, and elements according to the way {aggregates, bases, and elements were previously} explained in this treatise, having well analyzed (saṃpradhārya) their own characteristics (svalakṣaṇa). In this context {there are three. The first:

Some say: There are five including celibacy.
{Vaibhāṣika} masters indeed explain that celibacy merely dispels harm; it does not enhance.
Therefore, because something arises from those [causes], it arises from enhancement. The stream of enhancement surrounds the stream of karmic maturation {of those sense faculties} as if external to it, and through remaining there it thoroughly protects them. {Since food and so on enhance, they also benefit sense faculties that arise from karmic maturation.}
Objection: (1) If the five internal elements arise as natural outflow (niṣyandika),[391] then just two derivations would be untenable, but if not, then (2) it would contradict [the Abhidharmakośa,] which states, “The last three are momentary” (1:38b), and the Autocommentary, which explains:
Except for the first moment of the series, [commencing with] perseverance for Dharma knowledge of the truth of suffering,[392] there are no other conditioned phenomena not arising from natural outflow.[393]
Reply: Regarding those two objections, the latter error does not apply, since any later state derived from an earlier state of similar type exists as natural outflow (niṣyanda). And the first error does not apply because other than these two, there is no third type that arises from natural outflow. {If something distinct from them existed, it follows that one would observe the persistance of physical sense faculties in a corpse, just as material form and so on is observed to arise from natural outflow. But in general, though the karmic maturation of something ceases, it may remain due to enhancement, like a corpse. [74] And though the enhancement of something may no longer exist, its [karmic maturation] may remain, like the body of a hell being.}

From among the eighteen elements, that which is called “the phenomena element” is said to be homogeneous (sabhāga). {Any object that is ascertained as an object of any consciousness — which is an object with respect to which consciousness has arisen or will arise — is homogeneous. Objects or phenomena with respect to which mental consciousness neither arises nor will arise are nonexistent. In that case} [homogeneous objects] are appropriate for āryas or ordinary beings because they necessarily give rise to mental consciousness. Since self-cognition (svasaṃcitta) is not asserted in the [Vaibhāṣika] system, all phenomena are asserted to be objects of the mind discerning that “all phenomena are selfless” except for the consciousness engaging that object and its coexisting nonconcomitant states. But they would still be [objects of that mind] even if self-cognition were asserted, and this is the Vaibhāṣika position. In their system the phenomena element is always homogeneous, since all phenomena are the focal objects of the second moment of mental consciousness.[402]
{Objection: All eighteen elements are solely homogeneous because they are objects of the consciousness comprehending selflessness.
Reply: They are not posited as homogeneous since other specific [functions] are activated in the remaining [seventeen elements], and those whose [function] is not activated are partially homogeneous.}

Reply: That which is partially homogeneous is an element whose own discrete function is not activated (na svakarmakṛt), but that [whose specific function] is activated is homogeneous. Further, with respect to the six elements from the eye element to the mental element, it is impossible for two beings to look [at one object] through the same eyes and so on. Therefore that which is homogeneous for one [being] is homogeneous for all [beings], and that which is partially homogeneous for one [being] is partially homogeneous for all [beings]. {That homogeneous for one [being] is homogeneous for all [beings] since its specific function is activated through relying on others.
Objection: But because its specific function is not activated, such as [activating] seeing in others and so on, it is not homogeneous.
Reply: Its specific function is not solely to activate seeing in others, because many beings do not observe objects through the sense faculties and consciousness of one being. Therefore its function [of activating seeing] is posited for one mindstream, since it is not shared by all beings.}
One being can see, [hear, smell, taste, or touch] the five elements from form to tactile objects, such as the moon, a drama, a contest, or the sound of a drum, and it is possible that many beings see them and so on. Therefore, since they are common [to many observers], something is homogeneous in reliance on seeing and so on, and something is partially homogeneous in reliance on not seeing and so on. {Still, because a specific object is capable of generating consciousness in many beings, it can be posited from the perspective of many mindstreams.}

gal te gzugs sgra gnyis ma phrad par 'dzin pas de lta yin du chug na dri la sogs pa ni phrad nas 'dzin pas gang dag lhas byin gyi dbang pos 'dzin pa de dag mchod sbyin gyi dbang pos mi 'dzin pas thun mong ba ma yin pa'i phyir mig sogs dang mtshungs par 'gyur ro zhe na/ mi mtshungs te/ de dag ni gal te lhas byin gyi sna'i dbang po la sogs pa dang phrad na de'i sna'i rnam shes sogs skyed la/ 'on te mchod sbyin gyi sna'i dbang po la sogs pa dang phrad na de'i sna'i rnam shes la sogs pa skyed kyi gang zag gcig kho na'i sna'i rnam shes sogs skyed pa'i nges pa med ba'i phyir dang*/ sna'i nang na gnas pa'i srin bu phra mo la sogs pa'i sna'i rnam shes la sogs pa'ang skyed pa srid pas gang gi sna'i dbang po la sogs pa dang phrad pa de kho na'i sna'i rnam shes la sogs par skyed par nges pa med pa'i phyir ro/ /
des na mig la sogs pa ni thun mong ba ma yin pas rgyud gcig gi dbang gis bzhag la/ gzugs sogs lnga ni thun mong ba yin pas rgyud du ma'i dbang gis rnam par bzhag go zhes shes par bya'o/
{ 'o na chos kyang gang 'dzin pa'i rten bcas dang mi 'dzin pa'i de mtshungs min zhe na/ don lnga ni de lta ba kho na yul yin pas kha cig yul du ma byas par 'gag pa yod la/ chos ni dus gsum pa dang dus kyis ma bsdus pa thams cad yid shes skad cig gnyis kyi yul du 'gyur bas mi mtshungs so/ /}

{ nyon mongs can min ba'i phyir ro/ de nyid ji ltar yin ce na/} gal te so so'i skye bo nyon mongs can yin na 'dod pa'i so so'i skye bo 'jig rten pa'i lam gyis 'dod chags dang bral ba dag so so'i skye bo ma yin par thal ba'i phyir dang*/ {dge ba yin na/ } dge rtsa chad pa dag so so'i skye bo ma yin par 'gyur ba'i phyir dge ba'ang ma yin no/ /
des na so so'i skye bo ni ma bsgribs lung ma bstan yin no/ /
ngan song ba'i lus ngag gi las kyang gzugs yin pa'i phyir/ mthong spang min te/ de ni bden pa la log par mi rtog cing de'i rjes 'brang dang thob pa'ang ma yin pa'i phyir ro/{ des na gzugs dang dbang shes ni nyon mongs can yin yang mthong spang min no/ /}

mig de la brten pa'i rnam shes kho nas mthong ba ni mig te/ gang gi phyir zhe na/ rnam shes kyis mthong na de ni {sgrib pa la} thogs med yin pas rtsig pa la sogs pas bar du chod pa'i gzugs kyang mthong bar 'gyur na rtsig pa la sogs pa'i sgrib pas bar du chod pa'i gzugs ni mthong ba min pa'i phyir ro/ /
lo zhes bya ba ni mdo sde pas de zlog par byed de/ rnam shes kyis mthong yang bsgribs pa thams cad la 'jug par mi 'gyur te/ {mun pa la sogs/ } sgrib byed kyis bar du chod pa la rgyu ma tshang bas rnam shes mi skye ba'i phyir dang*/ rgyu tshang na{ mching bu dang} chu dang shel dang lhang tsher la sogs pas chod pa la'ang rnam shes skye ba'i phyir ro/{ rnam shes thogs med yin kyang chod pa'i yul mi mthong ste rkyen snang ba dang yid byed la sogs pa la ltos pa dang bcas pa'o/ /}
'o na mdo las mig gis gzugs rnams mthong nas zhes gsungs pa dang 'gal lo zhe na/ de ni mig la rten pa'i rnam shes kyis mthong ba la bshad pa yin te dper na khri'u dag 'bod do zhes brjod pa lta bu'o/ gal te mig{ gis gzugs mthong na re res mig shes re re skyed pa'i tshul} gcig gis mthong ngam{ 'on te/} gnyis kas{ mig shes gcig skyed pa'i tshul gyis} mthong zhe na/

Reply: Both eyes see form through generating a single {substance of} consciousness because if an object is viewed by one eye it does not see clearly, but if viewed with both eyes open, they see extremely clearly (vyakta). {Also, if it were that one eye alone sees, there would be no difference between first [seeing with one eye] then seeing [with both]}.
{If it is asserted that} it follows that consciousness ceases when its support ceases, {then it would follow that [the assertion of] matching substance — where the substance [of the object or basis] matches each substance of consciousness — would fail}. That [assertion] cannot be so; consciousness does not abide in its object, since it is not physical, {and the thousand eyes of Śakra and so on generate a single eye consciousness}. The term “also” indicates that either eye sees.
Objection: If the eyes and so on apprehend their objects through contact, {then near and distant objects would be untenable.} But {if they} do not {contact their object, then being impeded would be untenable.}

mig dang yid dang rna ba'i khams gsum ni yul dang ma phrad par 'dzin te/ {shel la sogs pas chod pa mthong ba'i phyir dang*/ } rgyang ring po'i gzugs sgra mthong ba dang thos pa'i phyir dang*/ yid kyis kyang 'das ma 'ongs dang thag ring po dag kyang 'dzin pa'i phyir dang*/ yid ni gzugs can ma yin pa'i yang phyir ro/{ yid ni gzugs can min pas yul dang mi phrad la mig kyang ma phrad pa 'dzin te phrad nas 'dzin na mig la byugs pa'i mig sman la sogs pa'ang mthong bar thal ba dang*/ thag ring po'i gzugs kyang phrad nas yun ring pos mthong bar 'gyur te/ mig de 'gro dang ldan gyur cig rgyang ring yun ring gis mthong 'gyur zhes so/ /
kha cig rna ba'i nang gi sgra thos pas rna ba phrad ma phrad gnyis ka'o zer mod kyi/ 'di ltar shar phyogs la sogs pa na grags pa'i sgras 'dir 'ong na rgyun dang ldan par 'gyur la/ shes pa der song na lus dang bral bar thal bas ma phrad par mngon no/ /
gzhan yang yul dang phrad pa yin na 'dod khams su bsam gtan pa rnams la lha'i mig rna ba'i mngon shes mi skye bar 'gyur te sna la sogs pa yul dang phrad nas 'dzin pas mngon shes mi srid pa bzhin no ma phrad na thag ring ba dang chod pa la sogs pa'i gzugs thams cad kyang mi 'dzin zhe na/ de ni phrad pa yang mtshungs te mig sman dang rang dang lhan cig skyes pa'i dri la sogs pa ji ltar mi 'dzin/ 'on kyang rung yul gzhan mi 'dzin pa ni nus pa'i phyad par te khab len gyis lcags 'dren pa bzhin no/ /}

mig gi rnam shes{ tsam po} de'i{ sa} gzugs ni sa thams cad du mthong ste/ rang sa dang 'og sa dang gong ma'i sa pa'i gzugs thams cad mthong ngo*/ /
de yang ci rigs kyi dbang du byas pa yin gyi khyad par ni 'di yod de/ 'dod pa'i mig shes kyis ni rang gi sa pa'i gzugs kho na mthong gi gong ma'i gzugs mthong ba ni med de/ de ni bskal pa'i don la mi 'jug pa'i phyir ro/ /
des na 'dir ni bsam gtan dang po'i mig shes kyis rang sa dang 'og sa dang gong ma'i sa pa'i gzugs mthong ba'i dbang du byas nas brjod do/{ de yang dang po'i mig shes bzhi pa'i mig la rten na lnga char gyi gzugs mthong ba'i phyir ro 'o na mchog dman pa'i yul du 'gyur ro zhe na skyon med de/ rten gong ma'i dbang po mchog yin pas mi ngan rgyal po mchog la rten nas bya ba bzang po sgrub pa bzhin no/}

Reply: The form of that {level’s ordinary} eye consciousness is seen on all levels because all form of its own level, lower levels, and higher levels is seen. But this is provisional because it excludes desire-realm eye consciousness, which sees only the form of its own level and not higher, since it doesn’t engage objects of higher status. Thus what is expressed here is from the perspective of eye consciousness of the first absorption, which sees the form of its level, lower levels, and higher levels.
{Objection: If the eye consciousness of the first level is supported by the eye of the fourth absorption, it would see form of the five types. But wouldn’t superior form then become inferior form?
Reply: There is no error because sense faculties reliant on higher realms are superior, like evil men who engage in pure conduct in reliance on a superior king.}

yang khams bco brgyad las{ rnam shes gang gi shes she na chos dang mig sogs gsal ba'i phyir} rnam shes gnyis kyis shes par bya ba ni gzugs nas reg bya'i bar phyi'i skye mched lnga {kho na }yin te/ gzugs ni mig gi rnam shes kyis shes par bya ba dang*/ yid kyi rnam shes kyis shes par bya ba yin pa la sogs pa'o/ /
