Those {desire-realm beings} possessing the faculty of pain possess seven: (1) body, (2) life force, (3) the mental faculty, (4) pleasure, (5) mental satisfaction, (6) equanimity, and (7) pain, because those free from attachment to the desire realm do not possess mental satisfaction {even though they are beings of the desire realm} and {desire-realm beings freed from attachment to sense objects} possess other types of feeling obtained through virtue or affliction.
Those possessing the female faculty (strīndriya) definitely possess eight because they possess (1–7) the seven previously explained, such as pain, and (8) the female faculty itself. The term “and so on” refers to the male faculty, mental dissatisfaction, and the five such as faith. The male faculty and mental satisfaction also possess eight: those [seven] previously explained plus itself. {Those who have not severed their root of virtue} possess (1–5) the five such as faith {possessed in all three realms}, (6) equanimity, (7) life force, and (8) the mental faculty. {In this context three faculties — male, female, and mental satisfaction — are subsumed by the desire realm.}
Those possessing the faculty of complete knowledge (ājñendriya) definitely possess eleven: (1) itself, (2) pleasure, (3) mental satisfaction, (4) equanimity, (5) life force, (6) the mental faculty, and (7–11) the five such as faith. Similarly, {nonlearners} who possess the faculty possessing complete knowledge (ājñājñātendriya) also definitely possess eleven: the ten previous faculties, and itself. {Since they also exist in the formless realm, they do not necessarily possess a body.}

Something is a mental factor (caitta) in that it instantly arises from the mind (citta), since a sūtra declares:
The phenomena of discernment and intention arise from the mind and depend on the mind.[500]
{In this regard, that which lacks a continuum of later moments arising from earlier moments of similar type is asserted to be light.}

The definition and classification of feeling (vedanā) has already been explained. {Yogācāra Levels states:
What function does feeling have? It functions to give rise to craving and to delineate equanimity.[501]}
{Therefore pleasure and pain} function to support craving {and, due to uncontaminated states and the latter [neutral feeling], are posited as equanimity}.

