Minds that are concomitant with {any of} the four root afflictions — (1) attachment, (2) anger, (3) pride, and (4) doubt — [127] or {any mental factors} that are specific-afflicted mental states, such as wrath and so on, or nonvirtuous regret, occur with twenty-one mental factors. Attachment {itself} and so on are separately added to the twenty previously mentioned, because those concomitant with attachment are not also concomitant with other root afflictions such as anger, except for ignorance. Those concomitant with anger and so on are not concomitant with other root afflictions such as attachment, except for ignorance. Also wrath and so on are concomitant with ignorance alone. They are not mutually concomitant with other root afflictions {because they are self-sufficient afflictions}, and ignorance has already been counted among afflicted mental states. The Compendium of Ascertainment mentions:
Attachment, anger, pride, and doubt, which are compatible with this mind, are not mutually concomitant because they are mutually incompatible.[523]
This explains that, except for ignorance, they are not mutually concomitant. The Compendium of Abhidharma indeed explains that attachment, pride, and view are concomitant; also anger and doubt are mutually concomitant.[524] But here it is not asserted that root afflictions other than ignorance are mutually concomitant. {If they were concomitant, then it would follow that the mind concomitant with the four mental factors of attachment and so on would occur with twenty-two mental factors. Therefore it is explained to occur with twenty-one.}

