Also, the obtainment of learner attributes {of the truth of path belongs to} learners, and the obtainment of nonlearner attributes {of the truth of path} belongs to nonlearners. The obtainment of neither learner (śaikṣa) nor nonlearner (aśaikṣa) attributes (dharma) has three types: (1) those belonging to learners, (2) those belonging to nonlearners, and (3) those belonging to neither {since there is no obtainment of space and states not conjoined with the mindstream, but there is obtainment of the two cessations and those conjoined with the mindstream}. Learner and nonlearner attributes are the truth of path, and that which is neither is contaminated and unconditioned phenomena. Further, since the obtainment of contaminated states, the obtainment of nonanalytical cessation, and the obtainment of analytical cessation are obtained by non-āryas, they are neither learner nor nonlearner obtainment. {The transcendent path} obtained by learners who obtain analytical cessation belongs to learners. That obtained by nonlearners {through the ninth liberated transcendent path that abandons the summit of existence} belongs to nonlearners.
{Objection: The assertion that the obtainment of analytical cessation by a nonlearner belongs to a nonlearner is illogical, because “nonlearner” refers to the liberated path and not to a path that frees from objects to be abandoned, and a nonlearner who obtains analytical cessation obtains abandonment of the least of the small objects to be abandoned by [the path of] meditation of the summit of existence by means of vajra-like concentration. Therefore it is learner obtainment and not nonlearner obtainment.
Reply: That is true. But it is called “nonlearner obtainment” since the uninterrupted path supports the obtainment of separation initiated by the uninterrupted path.}

