'on kyang rgyu rkyen dang tshogs pas yin no zhes bye brag tu smra ba zer mod kyi skye ba don gzhan ni bkag zin to/{ zhe na
'o na rgyu rkyen tshogs na des 'bras bu bskyed pas de dag rgyu yin gyi skye ba don gzhan yod kyang rgyu rkyen ma tshogs na mi skyed pas de rgyur 'dod pa don med do yod de dbang sogs bzhin/}

{Question: Is [sound] comprised of letters or syllables as its components?
Vaibhāṣikas say: It is not, for letters such as ka clarify [meaning] for the mental faculty alone. Nouns, predicated phrases, and letters are not sound because the letters ka and so on — which exist in the form of syllables — function to clarify meaning, but mere sound does not clarify meaning. Therefore speech engages nouns that clarify or generate [meaning,] and in expressing the meaning of nouns, [that meaning] is comprehended.
Sautrāntikas say: Mere sound is not speech. But when a speaker applies sound that is a conventional symbol, such as the term vase and so on, to an object, the object is understood. Therefore, what need is there to say that the name of the comprehended object and so on is imputed by speech?}

