{In general, instances such as vases, which represent conditioned states, have eight simultaneous characteristics — (1–4) the four primary characteristics and (5–8) the four secondary characteristics — that act as coemergent causes. Yet even though the four secondary characteristics simultaneously occur with their characterized basis and its three associated characteristics, [the secondary characteristics] are not coemergent causes that generate them, because they are like the outer servants of a king who are unable to directly serve the king and they do not generate three associated characteristics.
Though the mind and the associates of the mind are causes of each other, all secondary characteristics are not coemergent causes of all the four characterized bases because they are like outer servants. Form, sound, smell, taste, tactility, and the seven [actions] abandoned in pratimokṣa vows coexist with the four primary elements, but the elements belong to them; they do not belong to the primary elements. Further, the eight qualities of a conditioned vase, such as its arising but excluding itself, act as coemergent causes. However, the arising of arising acts as a coemergent cause that generates primary arising alone, [and that applies] up to the disintegration of disintegration, which generates primary disintegration alone.
The characteristics of mental factors, the two vows, and the mind and associates of the mind are associates of the mind because they are associated with the mind in ten ways. The ten ways are the four when their time is the same: in general, (1) time itself is the same because when the mind is established, [associates of the mind] are established, and specifically, (2) they arise at the same time, (3) they endure at the same time, and (4) they cease at the same time. There are three when their result is the same: (5) the mere result is the same, since the result of conscious effort (puruṣakāraphala) and result of separation (visaṃyogaphala) are the same but the dominant result (adhipatiphala) is omitted since it is common to all phenomena. (6) The result of karmic maturation is the same since the maturational result of the mind is also their [maturational result]. (7) The result of natural outflow (niṣyandaphala) is the same since the natural outflow [of the mind] is also their [natural outflow]. There are three (8–10) where if the mind is virtuous, its associates [165] are also virtuous, [nonvirtuous,] and so on.}

{Since they are omnipresent they cannot be causes of omnipresent states; since they are of different type they are not homogeneous causes; since they are not concomitant causes they do not occur simultaneously. Therefore} those [afflicted states] existing in the three times that are concomitant with nonomnipresent objects to be abandoned by seeing the truth of origin — as well as afflicted states to be abandoned by seeing and meditating on the truth of path and truth of cessation — are generated from the omnipresent cause of the afflicted view of the self.
The afflicted view of the self is not their result because they are not its omnipresent cause, {since omnipresent affliction is not asserted to be separate from the truth of suffering or the truth of origin.} And they are not homogeneous but distinct in type, {for it is said, “its own type and level, and those previously arisen” (2:52b).} Also they are not concomitant, and neither do they exist simultaneously. {Therefore it is impossible these four are its causes.}
So too, obtainment {in the three times} of all afflicted states, such as the afflicted view of the self and so on, {is also afflicted. Therefore it is the possibility of being a result but not a cause of the afflicted view of the self because obtainment of its function occurs later. And though it does not engage Dharma and is coemergent, it lacks potency. Therefore it is not a cause.} Except for {the three times}, the afflicted view of the self, and the characteristics of those concomitant with them, all characteristics of other afflicted states are any type of past or present homogeneity or the result of the omnipresent cause of the afflicted view of the self. They are not causes.

{The five virtuous aggregates are homogeneous with themselves, and the afflicted aggregates and the unobscured-neutral aggregates are each homogeneous with themselves.
Regarding neutral [aggregates,] some say: form is homogeneous with five aggregates since it is inferior,[616] but the other four are not homogeneous with form since they are superior to form.
In the same life, the kalala embryo is homogeneous with the ten stages of the embryo, but the stage of elder is homogeneous with its later stages alone. For those born human from a [previous] human birth, the stage of elder is homogeneous with the ten stages. For external states, barley is homogeneous with barley and so on. For internal states, eyes are homogeneous with eyes and so on, and the right eye is homogeneous with the right eye and so forth. Due to this, even those that occurred in the past are taught to be equal in status to those that will occur in future, and their cause is homogeneous since it is impossible for such states to have other causes.}

That is true not only for uncontaminated states, but also all contaminated virtue arising from preparation (prayogajā) is the homogeneous cause of both equal and superior states alone.
Question: What are they?
Reply: That derived from hearing (śrutamaya) is a homogeneous cause of the three: (1) itself, (2) that derived from contemplation (cintāmaya), and “and so on” refers to (3) that derived from meditation. That derived from contemplation is a homogeneous cause of both itself and that derived from meditation. That derived from meditation is a homogeneous cause of itself. {This should similarly be applied to four neutral states and from four to one desire-realm emanations. Indirectly this teaches that virtue obtained from birth and afflicted states are shared homogeneous causes. The afflicted homogeneity of smaller virtue obtained from birth is, for instance, the time when severing the root of virtue is abandoned by its antidote.}

Conditioned states (saṃskṛta) have three and a half results: karmic maturation, natural outflow, dominant result, and the result of conscious effort that is generated. Unconditioned states have {one and a half:} the result of conscious effort that is obtained and the result of separation (savisaṃyoga), which is analytical cessation established by countering contaminated states. These are results (phala) because the treatise (Topic Divisions)[626] declares:
What are resultant phenomena? All conditioned phenomena and analytical cessation.[627]
Therefore unconditioned states have one and a half results. {Space is devoid of actions and agents, and it is not an object of obtainment. Nonanalytical cessation is not a result since it is obtained merely by conditions being incomplete.}
Objection: Thus analytical cessation must be described as having a cause because it is an unconditioned state and also a result. It must also be described as a result because unconditioned states are asserted to be active causes. {And if those exist, it must be a conditioned state.}

Any result arising due to the power (bala) of its cause is called “a result arisen from conscious effort” (puruṣakāraphala), just as the obtainment of analytical cessation due to the path is called “that obtained by its power.” Because the cause accords with conscious effort [required to produce] the result, it is called “conscious effort,” like yellow hawk’s foot medicine or black crow’s foot medicine.[631]
Also, some assert: It arises immediately after or at the same time as its cause.
{Further, (1) results generated through conscious effort} are like a mind linked with a lower level producing concentration of a higher level, or the path of seeing arising after the supreme mundane state and so on, or a result occurring from coemergent causes, {or like the emanating mind generated by the mind of absorption. (2) Results obtained through conscious effort are described in [the statement that] “any result obtained by the power of its cause is a result obtained from conscious effort,” because it is like stating, “My wealth has grown,” where the obtainment of wealth is described in terms of the being [who achieves it]. The abandonment of that which is to be abandoned by the path of seeing is both a result of conscious effort and a result of separation for ārya serial abandoners, but it is a result of conscious effort alone [177] for āryas previously freed from attachment.}

This is stated for minds and mental factors.
Other states should be understood in just that way
except for concomitant cause. (2:61abc)

de'i don ni so sor thar pa'i sdom pa blangs nas gal te mnyam par ma bzhag na ni so sor thar pa'i sdom pa kho na 'byung la/ gal te des zag bcas kyi bsam gtan la mnyam par bzhag na bsam gtan gyi sdom pa 'byung la/ 'on te{ mnyam par ma bzhag pa dang zag bcas dang} zag med kyi bsam gtan la snyoms par zhugs na{ rim pa ltar so so thar pa kho na dang bsam gtan dang} zag med kyi sdom pa'i gzugs 'byung bas 'chol ba yin no zhes bya ba yin mod kyi de ni sems dang sems byung la'ang mtshungs te/ 'dod pa na spyod pa'i sems{ dang} sems byung las res 'ga' 'dod pa na spyod pa dang*/ res 'ga' gzugs na spyod pa dang*/ res 'ga' zag med kyi sems{ dang} sems byung{ la nyer gnyis sogs kyang} 'byung ba'i phyir ro/ /
de'i phyir snga ma dag legs par mngon no/ /
de'i phyir mtshungs pa ni{ tshor ba ris gnyis mi 'byung ba la sogs} rdzas mtshungs pa dang*/ de ma thag pa ni 'bras bu de ma thag tu skyed pa'o/{ mi ldan pa'i 'du byed rnams kyang 'chol te khams gsum pa dang*/zag med rnams dus cig car mngon du 'gyur bas so/ /}

de yang skyes pa{ 'das da ltar} rnams kho na yin gyi ma 'ongs pa ni ma yin te/ ma 'ongs pa la snga phyi med pa'i phyir dang*/{ da ltar ba da ltar ba'i de ma thag rkyen min pa bzhin no/ /}
de ma thag pa'i rkyen yang snga phyi la rnam par bzhag pa'i phyir ro/ /

'byung ba rnams kyi rgyu{ 'i rkyen} 'byung ba las gyur pa de yin na rnam smin gyis rgyu rnam pa gcig pu nyid byed do/ /
de yang{ lus ngag gi las} rig byed dang rig byed ma yin pa dge ba zag bcas dang mi dge ba dag gis skye ba phyi ma'i rnam smin skyes kyi dbang po'i tshogs pa na yod pa'i 'byung ba skyes pa yin no/ /
byed rgyu'i yod mod kyi de ni bye brag med par 'jug pa'i phyir rtag tu mi bgrang ngo zhes 'grel pa las 'byung ngo*/{ bdag rkyen ni rtogs sla bas ma bshad do/ /}

That derived from the elements is the causal {condition of} those elements in one way (ekadhā): the cause of karmic maturation. Further, indicative and nonindicative contaminated virtuous and nonvirtuous states give rise to {physical and verbal karma,} and that gives rise to the result of karmic maturation of a later life by generating the elements existing in the collection of the sense faculties. Though active cause exists, the Autocommentary notes:
It is never enumerated because it has no subsets.[652]
{The fact that the dominant condition is not explained is easy to comprehend.}

{The sixth,} when oppressed by afflicted meditative attainment, refers to a virtuous mind of lower level that occurs from a neutral obscured mind of higher level, since it is said, “As well as one pure state of lower level” (8:16b). Also, when rising from afflicted form-realm concentration, both virtuous minds of the desire realm occur, and when rising from afflicted formless concentration, only a form-realm virtuous mind derived from preparation occurs.
{When entering emanation, desire- and form-realm emanation minds occur from a virtuous form-realm mind derived from preparation and the emanating mind itself, since it is said, “That arises from pure absorption, and itself. Two arise from that” (7:50cd). When rising from emanation, the emanating mind itself and a form-realm mind derived from preparation occur from desire- and form-realm emanation minds.[654]
Some say that if these two occasions[655] are included in the occasions of entering and rising from meditative attainment, indeed there would be six occasions. But if those two are separated there, it would be acceptable to posit eight occasions.}

mi slob pa de las ni sems bzhi dag 'byung ste/ snyoms 'jug las ldang na khams gsum gyi dge ba snga ma ltar 'dod pa'i gnyis ka dang khams gong ma gnyis kyi sbyor byung gnyis te gsum dang*/ rgyun gyi tshe{ zad shes las mi skye ba shes pa lta bu} mi slob pa rang nyid 'byung ba'i phyir ro/ /
gal te de lta na mi slob pa las sems lnga 'byung bar 'gyur te/ yongs su nyams pa'i tshe mi slob pa'i mjug thogs su slob pa 'byung ba'i phyir zhe na/ nyon mongs pa mngon du ma byas par ni mi slob pa las mi nyams la/ nyon mongs mngon du byas pa na nyon mongs pas bar du chod pa'i phyir ro/{ de ma thag rkyen min pa'i phyir/ /}
de nyid kyi phyir TI ka byed dag gis kyang mi slob pa'i mjug thogs su slob pa ni mi 'byung ste/ yongs su nyams pa'i tshe'ang nyon mongs pa kun tu 'byung bas bar du chod pa'i phyir ro zhes bshad do/ /
'on kyang khams gsum gyi bsgribs pa'i sems gsum dang bdun 'byung bar thal bar 'gyur ba'i nyes pa ni spang dgos so/{ 'o na de'i mjug thogs sems brgyad 'byung bar thal dge ba gsum mi slob pa rang nyid/ de las nyams na khams gsum gyi nyon mongs gsum/ slob pa dang brgyad 'byung bas so/ /
zhe na/ nyon mongs can dang slob pa de'i de ma thag tu mi 'byung ste mi slob las ma langs par ni mi nyams la/ langs pa na dge sems las langs dgos pas nyon mongs dang slob pa'i bar du dge bas chod pa'i phyir ro/ /}
de dag ni sems bcu gnyis kyi de ma thag pa'i rkyen bshad pa'o/

The remaining {six} minds — {namely, (1–2) desire-realm and formless-realm virtuous minds, (3) a nonlearner mind, and (4–6) the three unobscured-neutral minds of the three realms} — obtain just these, but not others.
{Some say: That mind is obtained from before.
Others say: It is not obtained at that time.}

{ 'o na 'dod pa'i sprul sems la'ang 'dod pa'i 'dod chags rgyas par thal lo zhe na/
sprul pa'i dbang gis sprul sems la'ang chags pa'am 'dod pa kho na'i chos dri ro sprul pa ste sa gzhan gyi sprul sems gyis sa gzhan phyi sprul pa byed pa med pa'i phyir dang yang sprul pa byed pa gzhan las thos nas byed pas chags pa med kyang byed pa'am yang na rang gi sprul pa nyams na de la chags pa 'byung bas so/ /}
{de la de rgyas par mi 'gyur ro/ /}

de'i phyir 'gro ba ma bsgribs lung ma bstan du grub ste/ 'gro ba de dag 'grub pa'i rgyu las kyi srid pa las tha dad par gsungs pa'i phyir dang*/ lung sil bu las/ gnas brtan shA ri'i bus tshe dang ldan pa dmyal ba'i zag pa rnams mngon du gyur pa las dmyal ba myong bar 'gyur ba'i las rnams byed cing sogs par 'gyur ro/ /
tshe dang ldan pa lus dang ngag dang yid kyi dkyil 'khor dang skyon dang snyigs ma de dag gi rnam par smin pa gzugs dang tshor ba dang 'du shes dang 'du byed dang rnam par shes pa ni dmyal ba dag tu smin par 'gyur te/ rnam par smin pa grub nas dmyal ba zhes bya ba'i grangs su 'gro'o/ /
tshe dang ldan pa de la chos de dag las ma gtogs pa dmyal ba ma gtogs so zhes gsungs pa'i phyir te/ las kyis 'phangs pa'i rnam smin gyi phung po ni 'gro bar bshad pa'i phyir ro/{ de'ang rnam smin kho nar 'dod pa dang*/ rgyas byung yod par 'dod pa yod de/}

Therefore reincarnating beings are established as unobscured-neutral phenomena because it is said that reincarnating beings are distinct from the karmic existence that is the cause establishing them. It is written in the Miscellaneous Discourses:
The elder Śāriputra said: “Venerable, those who fall into hell manifest that state through having committed and accumulated karma to be experienced in the hells. Venerable, the cause of the karmic maturation of error and decline — created within the maṇḍala of body, speech, and mind — matures as the form, feeling, discernment, formation, and consciousness of a hell being, and when that karmic maturation is established, one is counted as a hell being. Venerable, a hell being is not observed to be other than those things.”[692]
That is stated because the maturational aggregates projected by karma are said to be the reincarnating being. {Further, they are said to be the result of karmic maturation alone and they exhibit enhancement.}

de ni{ rigs bzang bar rlam pa'i shAkya} nye du rnams chos la gzung ba'i phyir dang*/ 'khor los bsgyur ba'i rigs su shes nas gzhan de la rim gro bskyed du gzhug pa'i phyir dang*/ mi rnams phal cher mngal nas skye ba dang rjes su mthun par mdzad pa'i phyir ram/ yang na mir gyur pa dag gis kyang grub pa 'di 'thob bo zhes gdul bya rnams spro ba bskyed pa'i phyir ro/ /
gzhan yang {rigs dang gdung med na } mu stegs can gcer bu pa gnyen gyi bu la sogs pa dag kha zer ba'i sgyu mar rtog pa spang pa dang*/ sku gdung ring bsrel bzhag pa'i phyir ro/{ rdzus skyes shi ba rnams kyi lus mi gnas pas de mi rung bas so zhes zer ro/ /}
des na skye gnas thams cad kyi nang na rdzus skyes kho na mang ste/ de ni 'gro ba {lha dmyal } gnyis dang{ gzhan} gsum gyi phyogs dang srid pa bar ma pa thams cad yin pa'i phyir ro/ /

srid pa bar ma de ni sngon dus kyi srid pa 'byung bar 'gyur ba'i sha tshugs dang cha byad can yin te/ 'byung bar 'gyur ba'i sngon dus kyi srid pa dang bar srid gnyis 'phen pa'i las gcig las skyes pa'i phyir ro/ /
'o na khyi mo la sogs pa'i mngal du gnas pa dag shi ste dmyal ba'i srid pa bar ma grub na mngal dang de na gnas pa gzhan dag kyang 'tshig par 'gyur te/ de'i sngon dus ni me 'bar ba'i rnam pa can yin la/ bar srid gang du shi ba de nyid du 'grub pa'i phyir ro zhe na
mi 'gyur te/ rang rang gi las kyis mtshams bcad pa'i phyir dang*/ srid pa bar ma shin tu dang ba yin pas bltar mi rung ba bzhin du reg tu'ang mi rung ba'i phyir ro/{ de'i kha dog ni lung phran tshegs la sogs pa las dmyal ba dang dud 'gro dang yi dwags dang 'dod pa'i lha mi dang gzugs khams pa rnams kyi bar srid ni rim pa ltar sdong du ma 'tshig pa dang du ba dang chu dang gser lta bu dang dkar por gsungs shing*/ /}

The intermediate existence has the manner and appearance that will exist for the prior existence (pūrvakālabhava) because both the prior existence that will arise and the intermediate existence are born from the same projecting (ekākṣepa) karma.
Objection: Now, if those abiding in the wombs of female dogs and so on die and establish the intermediate existence of a hell being, that abode and those abiding there would be incinerated because its prior existence possesses the aspect of blazing fire and that is established for the intermediate existence in just that place of death.
Reply: That does not occur because the karma of each is separated, and because it cannot be seen or touched since the intermediate existence is completely diaphanous. {It is said in the Kṣudraka Collection and so on that the color of the intermediate existences of (1) hell beings, (2) animals, (3) ghosts, (4) desire[-realm] gods and humans, and (5) form-realm gods are respectively like (1) a burned tree stump, (2) smoke, (3) water, (4) gold, and (5) white.}

yang 'jug pa shes la phyi ma gnyis mi shes pa dang*/ dang po gnyis shes la 'byung ba mi shes pa dang*/ gsum ga shes pa zhes bya ba'i mngal du 'jug pa ni gsum dag ste/ de la 'khor los bsgyur ba'i rgyal po ni dang po dang*/ rang byung rang rgyal dang{ rdzogs pa'i} sangs rgyas bcom ldan 'das gnyis ni gnyis pa dang gsum pa yin no/ /
ci'i phyir zhe na/
'khor los bsgyur ba{ 'i bar srid} ni{ bsod nams kyi} las rgya che ba'i phyir dang*/ rang sangs rgyas ni bskal pa brgyar goms{ shing dngos po drug shes} pa'i ye shes rgya che ba'i phyir dang*/ sangs rgyas ni bsod nams dang ye shes gnyis ka rgya che ba'i phyir na go rim bzhin du sbyar ro/{ bsgyur rang gnyis gnas skabs der ma la sred pas sems nyon mongs can yin yang shes bzhin ni 'du shes dang mos pa phyin ci ma log pa la brjod do/ /}

Again, entry to the womb (garbha) has three types: (1) conscious when entering the womb but unconscious in the latter two phases, (2) conscious in the first two but unconscious when exiting, and (3) conscious in all three phases. Cakravartin kings are the first, and the two self-arisen (svayaṃbhuva) beings — pratyekabuddhas and {perfect} buddhas — are the second and the third, respectively.
Question: Why is that?
Reply: That is so because respectively {the intermediate existence of a} cakravartin has extensive {meritorious} karma, that of a pratyekabuddha has extensive transcendent wisdom (jñāna) {understanding the six things} cultivated for over one hundred eons, and that of a buddha has extensive accumulations of both merit and transcendent wisdom. {Regarding the intermediate beings of both cakravartins and pratyekabuddhas, their minds are afflicted since they crave their mother, and their conscious entry is described as distorted discernment and resolution.}

ma rig pa ni skye ba sngon{ tshe snga} ma la nyon mongs pa spyod pa'i gnas skabs kyi phung po lnga{ ste nyon mongs} yin no/{ ma rig pa ni 'phen byed yin pas mthong spang yin no zhes 'dod kyang bden pa ma mthong ba la sgom spang gi ma rig pas kyang 'phen pa dang mthong spang rdzogs byed kyi las slong ba mi 'gal/ mthong nas 'phen pa ni 'khor ba la sred pa log pa'o/ /
zhes 'chad do/ /
slob dpon thogs med mthong sgom gnyis ka'i spang byar 'dod do/ /}

'du byed dag ni skye ba sngon{ tshe snga} ma la{ bsod nams} dge ba la sogs pa'i las{ gsum} spyod pa'i gnas skabs kyi phung po lnga{ ste las so} 'o rnam shes ni skye ba 'dir ma'i mngal du nying mtshams sbyor ba'i skad cig gis phung po lnga yin no/{ rnam shes dang skye ba gnyis skad cig ma re re la gzhan rnams rgyun chags pa yin no/ /}
ming dang gzugs ni mtshams sbyar ba'i skad cig ma de phan chad nas gzung nas skye mched drug dod tshun chad de skye mched drug ma dod kyi bar te/ de la ming ni tshor ba dang 'du shes dang 'du byed dang yid kyi rnam shes yin te/ ming ltar thogs pa med pa'i phyir ro/ gzugs ni mer mer po la sogs pa'o/{ dod nas skye mched drug tu 'jog ste/ /}
skye mched drug po de ni yul dang dbang po dang rnam shes gsum 'dus nas yul la longs spyod pa tshun chad de skye mched {mngal gyi gnas skabs dang po bzhi yin no }drug dod nas dbang po dang yul dang rnam shes gsum ma 'dus kyi bar gyi mig la sogs pa dod pa'i gnas skabs {ste ma btsas kyi bar ro } so/ {gsum 'dus nas reg par 'jog ste } reg pa ni gsum 'dus nas yul yongs su gcod pa nas gzung ste bde ba dang sdug bsngal dang{ sogs pa} btang snyoms kyi rgyu yul yid du 'ong ba dang mi 'ong ba dang bar ma la blang dor shes nus pa tshun chad do/ /
de yang gsum 'dus nas tshor ba gsum gyi rgyu yongs su gcod par mi nus pa'i bar gyi phung po lnga{ste sdug bsngal gyi rgyur ma shes na me la reg pa'i dus sogs so/ /}'o/ /

gzugs kyi skye mched ni kham gyi zas ma yin te/ zas ni dbang po dang de'i rten 'byung ba la phan 'dogs pas yin na/ gzugs kyi skye mched des rang gi dbang po mig la mi phan pa'i phyir dang*/ dbang po'i rten gyi 'byung ba la'ang mi phan pa'i phyir ro/ /
de mid pa'i tshe rang gi dbang po dang de'i rten gyi 'byung ba la'ang phan mi 'dogs na gzhan gyi dbang po dang 'byung ba la ji ltar phan 'dogs par 'gyur/ gal te bkres pas nyen pas zas mthong ba tsam gyis kyang phan pa ma yin nam zhe na/ zas mthong ba tsam gyis yid bde ba ni bde ba myong bar 'gyur ba'i reg pa zas su byas pa yin gyi gzugs ni ma yin te/ gal te gzugs mthong ba tsam gyis yin na 'dod pa las 'dod chags dang bral ba phyir mi 'ong dang dgra bcom pa rnams kyis kyang zas mthong ba tsam gyis tshim par 'gyur na 'dod pa'i nyon mongs las rnam par grol ba rnams la mi phan pa'i phyir {da lta yang re ba dang bral ba'i zas mthong bas mi phan pa'i phyir } de'i tshe yang srid pa dang bcas pa'i{ sems pa'am} reg pa{ nyon mongs can} kho na zas su 'dod do/{ gang spel na re gzugs kham zas min te phrad nas 'dzin pa'i dbang po'i yul ma yin pa'i phyir sgra bzhin no zhes gsungs pa nyid legs so/ /
gzhan yid dang chos gzugs can min la/ dbang po lnga 'od ltar dang ba dang sgra la rgyun med pas kham zas ma yin zhes gsung ngo*/ /}

The Compendium of Ascertainment explains:
Through being received, it arises from being received;
through objects, it arises from objects;
through hope, it arises from hope;
through being conjoined, it arises from being conjoined.[818]
This passage definitely asserts four: (1) developing the body, (2) developing feelings, (3) developing the mind, and (4) developing mental factors. Or it refers to the close placement of mindfulness of the body and so on.
{If the four possibilities regarding that which is coarse [or divisible] and food are calculated, there are four possibilities: (1) The first possibility is that which is coarse but not food. This is, for instance, a blazing hammer and so on that does not benefit when either eaten or digested. (2) That which is not coarse but is food is contact, intention, and consciousness. (3) That which is both is any food that enhances the sense faculties along with their bases. (4) That which is neither is sound or all those concomitant with the feeling of pain.
Moreover, contact and food have four possibilities: (1) That which is contact but not food is uncontaminated contact. (2) That which is not contact but food is oral food, intention, and consciousness. (3) That which is both is contaminated contact. (4) That which is neither are those other than those previously mentioned.
The other three foods also have four possibilities.
Vaibhāṣikas say that since food has the two functions of being eaten and digested, even blazing hammers are food since they are capable of eliminating hunger and thirst when eaten.
The treatise considers such food to be the food of enhancement.[819]}
These explain the method of abidance. [246]

de'i rjes la{ las kyi dbang gis} nam mkha' la sa bon{ nam khams} sna tshogs kyi snying po can gyi sprin 'khrigs te char lo mang du bab pa las yang mtsho chen po zhig 'chags te/ tshad ni dpag tshad brgyad khri tsam zhig tu nges so/ /
de la khams rab sdud cing 'byed pa'i rlung gis gru bzhir dkrugs pa las gser gyi sa gzhi de la lhun po'i ri 'chags te/ brjid che bas na lhun po dang*/ ri'i rgyal po yin pas na ri rab bo/{ de bzhin du ri phyi ma rnams dang gling rnams kyang rang rang gi dbyibs dang 'dra ba'i rlung las rim gyis 'grub bo/ /}
yang khams 'bring sdud cing 'byed pa'i rlung gis gru bzhir ri de la bskor de dkrugs pa las lhun po'i phyi rol tu gnya' shing 'dzin dang*/ gshol mda' 'dzin dang*/ seng ldeng can dang*/ de bzhin blta na sdug gi ri dang*/ rta rna dang*/ rnam par 'dud dang*/ mu khyud 'dzin gyi ri zhes bya ba bdun chags te/ ri de dag gi rtse mo nyi ma'i shing rta'i gnya' shing 'dzin pa'am/ gnya' shing gi rnam pa dang*/ gshol mda'i rnam pa dang*/ {gser gyi seng ldeng yod pas sam } seng ldeng gi mdangs 'dra ba dang*/ yid du 'ong ba dang*/ brag rta'i rna ba 'dra ba dang*/ dbus phyogs su rnam par 'dud pa dang*/ thams cad kyi mtha' bskor ba'i phyir rim pa bzhin du de skad du brjod do/ /{de dag ni ri dgu yin la sa'i dngos gzhi las ni rnam par 'dud dang*/ rta rna ra dang*/ blta na sdug dang*/ seng ldeng can dang*/ gshol mda' 'dzin dang*/ zhes bar pa lnga go rim gzhan du bshad cing*/ ri de dag gi ming de rnams ni de dag gi rtse mo'i rnam pa 'dra ba'i bye brag las blta bar bya'o/ /
zhes bshad do/ /}

Then {through the power of karma} the source clouds of various seeds {or elements (dhātu)} collect in space, and it rains for many years, forming a large ocean measuring 80,000 yojanas. Condensing and separating winds churn the supreme-level elements into a square, and from that Meru forms on the foundation of gold. It is Meru since it has great splendor, and it is the supreme mountain since it is the king of mountains. {Similarly, the later mountains and elements are serially established from winds of like shape.}
Again, condensing and separating winds that circle Mount Meru churn the medium-level elements into a square so that beyond Meru the seven mountains called Yugandhara, Īṣādhara, Khadiraka, as well as Sudarśana, Aśvakarṇa, Vinitaka, and Nimiṃdhara form. They are given their names because the peaks of these mountains respectively resemble (1) the yoke of the sun chariot, or a yoke, (2) a plough beam, (3) a radiant khadira tree[841] {since it is like a golden khadira}, (4) something beautiful, (5) the ears of a rock horse, (6) something bowing from the middle, and (7) something that surrounds all. {These are included in the nine mountains. Yogācāra Levels serially lists five:
Vinataka, Aśvakarṇa, Sudarśana, Khadiraka, Īṣādhara.
And:
The names of these mountains should be viewed as specifically describing the shape of their peaks.[842]}

shing de'i shar du dpag tshad nyi shu 'das pa na rdzing bu dal gyis 'bab ces bya ba chu zheng du dpag tshad lnga bcu lnga bcu yod pa mtha' skor la dpag tshad nyis brgyas 'khor ba rdzing g.yog brgyad stong dang bcas pa yod do/ /
brgya byin gyi g.yul ngor spyod pa'i glang po che rab brtan ni glang g.yog brgyad stong dang bcas te dgun {dang dpyid dang dbyar } zla ba bzhi{ bzhir rim pa ltar} brag gser gyi bya skyibs lha ma yin gyi ngos dang dpyid zla ba bzhi sA la'i rgyal po rab brtan gyi drung dang*/ dbyar zla ba bzhi rdzing bu dal gyis 'bab tu gnas so/{ 'di'i lus kyi tshad ni srid du dpag tshad phyed dang gsum/ zheng du gcig pa mtha' bdun gyis 'khor ba/ 'phang du phyed dang gnyis pa ste/ lus dkar po mgo sngo la mgo bo'i dbus dmar ba mche ba drug dang ldan pa gnas gzhan du 'gro ba na tha mal pa'am/ lha'i rdzu 'phrul che ba gang rung du sprul nas glang po dag gi mchog la zhon nas sil snyan la sogs pa rgya cher byed cing 'gro'o/ /
der gnas pa na 'khor rnams kyis kha nang du ltas phreng ba rim bdun gyi sa la pad ma'i rtsa ba za'o/}

Yogācāra Levels explains that the Sañjīva hell exists 32,000 yojanas below {the snow mountains} and the other seven are spaced at intervals of 4,000 yojanas.[868] The abode of Avīci that is explained here and in other texts is 20,000 yojanas in height. But if both versions are compatible, then Avīci would be 80,000 yojanas below this continent, and its lower base would actually be the golden base.
In the Sañjīva hell, the karma of beings there causes various weapons {to appear} in their hands and compels them to judge others as enemies. Then they attack with their weapons and cut each other to pieces. They then collapse as if in a faint until {a cool wind stirs} and a voice proclaims from the sky, “Revive again!” whereupon they revive and fight each other once more. {The beings who are born here are those with karma derived from strong aversion, those who are spiteful, and those who kill human beings.} [259]
In the Kālasūtra hell, black lines are drawn on the bodies of sentient beings from their crowns to their feet. Then saws and so on are used to cut along these lines, and when the upper part is cut, the lower part heals, and when the lower is cut, the upper part heals. {Those who are born here possess aversion for the Three Jewels, their parents, śrāvakas, and pratyekabuddhas and are motivated to commit negative actions toward them: lie to them, slander them, and act with guile and deceit toward them.}

In the Saṃghāta hell, sentient beings are beaten with iron hammers and crushed and squeezed between iron mountains with peaks like ram’s heads. Later their dismembered bodies revive and again they are crushed. {Those who are born here are those who like to kill oxen, goats, sheep, and wild animals and are predominantly engaged in the three nonvirtuous actions of the body.}
In the Raurava hell, beings are terrified by the blazing iron ground, but when they enter a dense forest, it erupts in flame, and they are burned for many years. By the power of karma they have tongues a thousand yojanas long that are ploughed by oxen with iron horns and hooves pulling blazing iron ploughs. {Those who are born here are those who like to take the lives of others and drink their blood and those who deceive with measuring scales}. In the Mahāraurava hell, the suffering is twice that of Raurava. {Those who hold wrong views, [those with] great craving, and those who deceive are born here.}
In the Tapana hell, beings are born in a three-layered iron cauldron and boiled and burned by blazing fire. {Those born here are those who harm brahmans, parents, and many sentient beings.} In the Pratāpana hell, the suffering is twice that of Tapana. {Those who kill beings who are worthy of offerings are born here.}
In the hell of Avīci, one’s body experiences the flames and suffering of a single tongue of fire within an iron house. {Those who state that Dharma is non-Dharma, or that non-Dharma is Dharma, or hate holy beings, or advocate nihilism fall into Avīci.} These eight are called the eight hot hells since they have a predominance of the suffering of heat.

The gods of Trāyastriṃśa live on the summit of Meru.
Question: Why are they called that?
Reply: {There are thirty-three gods: the eight called the eight wealth gods (vasus): Pṛthivī, Agni, Antarikṣa, Vāyu, [271] Dyauṣ, Sūrya, Nakṣatra, Soma. There are eleven called the eleven fierce gods (rudras): Ānanda, Vijñāna, Manas, Prāṇa, Vāc, Īśāna, Tatpuruṣa, Aghora, Vāmadeva, Sadyojāta, Ātma. There are twelve suns (ādityas): Mitra, Aryaman, Bhaga, Varuṇa, Dakṣa, Aṃśa, Tvāṣṭṛ, Pūṣan, Vivasvat, Savitṛ, Śakra, Viṣṇu. And there are the two youthful sons of Aśvinī: Indra and Kārtikeya.}

{He thinks of the elephant Airāvata, and Airāvata and his retinue come from Jambudvīpa. They come manifesting thirty-two heads and seven tusks for each head, seven ponds, seven lotuses, seven devaputras, seven servants, and seven cymbals. They are ridden by Indra and Viṣṇu aggressively through the city, and through that aggressive behavior they become mentally and physically aggressive. [277] The four army batallions assemble with the resolution to fight the battle and brandish four types of weapons and armor and fight to the south of Meru. Those who are to be defeated fight three times, and in the place where they are to be defeated they grow cold, the gate is closed, and they become terrified.}
If at that time the demigods are victorious, they pursue the gods into the city of Sudarśana. {Then weapons and dismembered corpses fall into the ocean, and the blood of dead beings colors the ocean. Since just how the war will progress appears in the All-Seeing lake of the garland of stars, so relatives of the dead demigods mourn even before the war stops. This is explained in the Foundations of Mindfulness Sūtra.}
If the gods win, they pursue the demigods up to the first pleasure ocean. {The drum proclaims: “The gods have won, the demigods have been defeated, the animals have been put to flight and terrorized,” and the demigods return to their own abode.} If their heads or waists are severed, they die. If their major or minor limbs are severed, they grow back again.
The name Yāma (Free of Conflict) is given because beings there do not engage in such conflict. It is also called Twins because [in the Yāma heaven] both sons and daughters of the gods are born supernaturally in the laps of daughters of the gods. Their enjoyment is superior to that of the gods of Trāyastriṃśa.
They are called, respectively, Tuṣita (Joy), Nirmāṇarati (Enjoying Emanations), and Paranirmitavaśavartin (Controlling Others’ Emanations) because, respectively, beings there possess the joy of Mahāyāna Dharma, they experience joy through the sense objects of their own emanation, and they also experience joy in those emanated by others. The meanings of the names of the form and formless realms have been explained.
Question: What do those of the desire realm engage in?

mdo las/ 'dod pa skye ba gsum bshad pa'i 'dod pa skye ba gsum po ni 'dod pa'i lha dang mir bcas pa rnams yin te/ de la 'dod pa'i mi rnams dga' ldan gyi bar gyi lha'i ris bzhi ni 'dod pa nye bar gnas pa dag la dbang phyug gis dbang byed pa'i 'dod pa skye ba yin no/ /
{de yang rang gi rnam smin las ji ltar byung ba longs spyod pas so } 'phrul dga' ba ni rang gi sprul pa la dbang byed pa'i 'dod pa skye ba yin no/ /
{de yang ji ltar 'dod pa bzhin bdag nyid kyis sprul pa la longs spyod pas so } gzhan 'phrul dbang byed pa ni gzhan gyi sprul pa la dbang byed pa'i 'dod pa skye ba yin no/{ de yang ji ltar 'dod pa bzhin rang gzhan gnyis kas sprul pa la dbang byed pa'i pas so/ /}

Because the dimensions of bodies in the three lower realms is not fixed, they are not taught here. {Yogācāra Levels declares:
The dimensions of those in the great hells are not fixed, but the bodies of those who commit and accumulate intensely strong evil nonvirtuous karma are extremely large. Other beings have other outcomes. In just the way the dimensions of those in the great hells are established, so too are the cold hells, surrounding hells, occasional hells, animals, and ghosts.[946]
This explains that the size of the bodies [of hell beings] is determined by the size of the nonvirtue committed.}

The size of the bodies of humans of Jambudvīpa is four cubits for the larger and three and a half cubits for the smaller. The universal cubit is explained below, measured in terms of the accumulation of coarse and subtle particles and so on, but the [measure of a] cubit for each individual is not [explained here]. {Yogācāra Levels notes:
The measure of the bodies of humans of Jambudvīpa is not fixed because some are larger and some are smaller. Still, the measure for any individual is [approximately] three and a half cubits.[947]}
Those of eastern Videha, western Godānīya, and northern Kuru grow to twice the size of the former, respectively, eight cubits, sixteen cubits, and thirty-two cubits. These measurements are from the perspective of a cubit of Jambudvīpa, and each is just four {or three and a half} cubits. {Yogācāra Levels explains:
The dimensions of the bodies of eastern Videha are fixed because they are three and a half of their cubits and their bodies are extremely large. The bodies of western Godānīya and northern Kuru are also extremely large, like those of eastern Videha.[948]}
It is well known that the humans of the minor continents possess bodies half the size of the bodies of humans of the main continent. Master Pūrṇavardhana also explains:
Because all eight intermediate continents are established by inferior karma, humans abiding there have smaller bodies.[949]

Twenty-four finger widths are a cubit;
four cubits are one full armspan;
five hundred of those are a krośa;
beyond the village is considered the site for a monastery. (3:87)

dngos po la de ltar yin kyang{ shin tu phra zhib che bas} lo gsum gsum na 'dor ba ni rgyal po dang mthun par bya ba'i phyir yin pas nyes pa med de/ {lung sde bzhi'i gzhung dam pa la zhu ba dang*/ 'dul byed dang*/ gcig las 'phros pa dang*/ lnga tshan dang*/ bcu drug tshan dang*/ gleng gzhi dang*/ rkyang pa'am sil bu dang*/ gtam gyi gzhi'am dngos po dang*/ ma na bi ka'am/ bram ze'i bu mo lta bu bsnyen par rdzogs pa'i phung po rab tu ldan pa'i phung po bya ba'i phung po gsum ston pa dang*/ ma mo lta bu dang*/ lus la sogs pa dmigs pa dang*/ byin rlabs dang*/ mtshan nyid dang*/ gleng gzhi dang*/ ngo bo dang*/ 'bras bu dang bdun ston pa ste le'u lag bcu gcig yod pa las drug pa gleng gzhi la sgo lnga yod pa las/ sgo dang po bzhi la bsdoms tshigs bcu bcu/ sgo lnga pa la bsdoms kyi tshigs drug yod pa las gleng gzhi'i sgo dang po'i bsdoms tshigs tshigs bcad gnyis pa'i skabs las ston pa mnyan yod kyi rgyal byed kyi tshal na bzhugs pa'i tshe dge slong rnams la lo lnga lnga na bshol dor cig ces gsungs nas lo lnga lnga na bshol 'dor ro/ /
rgyal po dag ni lo gsum gsum na bshol 'dor bar byed do/ /
