Reply: That is true, {but alternate calculations are complex}. There is no fault in allocating [the intercalary month] every three years because this conforms with the system of the king.
{The holy text of the Chapters on Vinaya teaches: supplication, taming, elaboration, the five sections, the sixteen sections, ethical precedents, prose, and the basis or state of language; it devotes three chapters to full ordination in the part [called] Manavika or “the brahman maiden”; and [the final chapter] presents seven things: the mātṛkā (point summaries), focal objects such as the body, blessings, characteristics, ethical precedents, nature, and result. From the eleven chapters, the sixth on ethical precedents has five divisions. The first four divisions have ten verses each as summary, and the fifth division has six verses as summary. Regarding two verses of the summary of the first division of the ethical precedents, it is said:
When the Teacher was dwelling at Jetavana in Śrāvastī, he said: “Monks, allocate the intercalary [month] every five years.”[974] Therefore the intercalary month was allocated every five years. In the system of the king, the intercalary [month] is allocated every three years. The monks did not act in accordance with the system of the king and censured the ministers, and having done that they informed the Bhagavan. The Bhagavan said: “Monks, censuring the ministers is boastfulness; you should act in accordance with the injunction of the king.”}

Then beings of Uttarakuru are born as desire-realm gods, since they are obscured by karmic maturation and cannot generate concentration, and Uttarakuru becomes empty. Then the desire gods generate the first absorption spontaneously. {Words similar to those previously stated are proclaimed, all beings obtain the first absorption, die,} and are reborn in the first absorption. {That is the destruction of the desire realm.}
Then beings of the first absorption obtain the concentration of the second absorption {in the same way as before}. {At the time of destruction by fire, one being in the heaven of Brahma generates the second absorption spontaneously and twice proclaims, “Listen, joy and pleasure that are generated from nonattachment are blissful,” and others hear that, and by entering that bliss they die and are reborn in the second absorption. In the destruction by fire, sentient beings are destroyed just up to that level. In the destruction by water, gods of the second absorption generate the third absorption and so too are born in the third absorption. In the destruction by wind, gods of the third absorption generate the fourth absorption and are born in the fourth absorption.}
The destruction of sentient beings takes nineteen intermediate eons.

Further, buddhas appear during the decrease of lifespan from 80,000 years to a hundred years but not when more than 80,000 because formation is not yet complete. Buddhas do not appear during periods of increase because sentient beings are not disappointed with saṃsāra, or during the period of decrease after a hundred years, because the five degenerations of (1) lifespan, (2) time, (3) afflictions, (4) view, and (5) sentient beings manifest intensely. {(1) The degeneration of lifespan is the decline of life force. (2) The degeneration of time is the decline of objects of enjoyment where results, taste, potency, and strength diminish. (3) The degeneration of afflictions is the decline of the virtuous state of householders. (4) The degeneration of view is the decline of the virtuous state of the ordained. (5) The degeneration of sentient beings is the deterioration of the body. These are degenerations because size, form, freedom from disease, strength, intelligence, mindfulness, effort, and stability degenerate. They are degenerations since they are states of evil decline.}
Also, these principles of degeneration are from the perspective of this eon, but for other eons there is no certainty. {Ornament of the Sage’s Thought declares:
Some eons are set at eighty intermediate eons, but this is not one because it is extremely long due to the power of prayer and the root of virtue. To use an analogy, [such] eons are [hard to measure] like the stars because the duration of an intermediate eon [292] of decrease is said to be 20,000 eons.}[989]

gser gyi 'khor lo can ni{ khams kyi rgyal phran rnams kyis} phas bsu ba byed pa dang*/ dngul gyi 'khor lo can ni rang 'gro ba dang*/ zangs kyi 'khor lo can ni g.yul bshams pa tsam dang*/ lcags kyi 'khor lo can ni mtshon cha brtsams pa tsam gyis pha rol pos 'dud cing rgyal bar byed kyang sems can 'ga'i yang srog la gnod pa med de/ des mi dge ba bcu spangs pa'i phyir ro/ /
{de yang dus mnyam du sems bskyed cing tshogs bsags pa dag la kha cig sangs rgya la kha cig la gegs byed pa su yod/ des na zhing gcig tu lhan cig mi 'byung yang gzhan du 'byung ngo / / mdo de ni zhing gcig la dgongs pa ste/ gzhan du na 'khor los bsgyur ba'ang gcig las mi 'byung bar mtshungs so zhes zer ro/ / de na skye ba'i rgyu ni sngar ma bzhengs pa'i sar mchod rten rtsig pa dang glegs bam bzhengs pa dang chos 'khor byed pa la sogs pa yin no/ /}
de bzhin du 'khor los bsgyur ba de dag la yo byad phun sum tshogs pa rin po che gzhan drug kyang yod de/ glang po dang*/ rta dang*/ nor bu dang*/ bud med dang*/ khyim bdag dang*/ blon po rin po che ste rgyal srid rin po che bdun dang*/ dran pa nyer gzhag gi mdo las/ ral gri dang*/ pags pa dang*/ mal cha dang*/ chal dang*/ khyim dang*/ gos dang*/ lham nye ba'i rin po che bdun yang 'byung bar gsung la/

That intermediate eon ends with the eon of weapons (śastra), the eon of sickness (rogā), and the eon of famine (durbhikṣa). Famines have three types: the box famine (cañcu) {where grain is placed in boxes}, the white-bone famine {where the bones have no fat}, and the spoon famine {that holds a mere grain}. The eons where beings meet their end through weapons, sickness, or famine endure, respectively: seven days; seven months and seven days; and seven years, seven months, and seven days. The cause of liberation from such intermediate eons is refraining daily from taking life, offering medicine to the Saṅgha, and offering food to the Saṅgha because the Upper Room Sūtra declares:
Ānanda, such intermediate eons are threefold. What are these three? The intermediate eon of weapons, the intermediate eon of sickness, the intermediate eon of famine. Ānanda, whoever abandons taking life for one day, and lives correctly practicing [298] that, will not be born in the intermediate eon of weapons. Whoever offers the medicine of just one arura[1010] plant to the Saṅgha will not be born in the intermediate eon of sickness. Whoever offers just a ladle of food to the Saṅgha will not be born in the intermediate eon of famine.[1011]
{Yogācāra Levels explains that famine occurs for thirty eons, sickness for twenty eons, weapons for ten eons. At those times, harmful intent increases, complexion worsens, the body weakens, and hunger and thirst increase in two continents. But this does not occur at all in the northern continent of Kuru. The seed of formation of the gross environment exists within the wind maṇḍala of the peak degree of destruction, and it also exists where it is carried by other mundane winds.[1012]}

{skye ba'i } bsam gtan gsum po de'i nang gi skyon rim pa ltar rtog dpyod dang dga' ba dang dbugs 'byung rngub me chu rlung gsum dang chos mthun pa'i phyir ro/ /
bsam gtan bzhi pa na ni{ snod la} phyi rol gyi 'jig pa'i rgyu med de/ gnas brgyad par 'chad pa'i skyon brgyad kyis mi g.yo ba'i phyir ro/{ gcig gnas gtsong ma rnams kyi mthu yin te/ de dag ni lhag mthong spyod pas gzugs med du 'jug mi nus la/ gzhan du yang mi 'gro ste/ phyir mi 'ong rnams khyad par du 'gro ba'i phyir ro zhes zer ro/ /}
'o na bzhi pa'i snod de dag rtag par 'gyur ro zhe na/

Reply: That is because the intrinsic errors of those three absorptions {where beings are born} are, respectively, (1) investigation and analysis, (2) joy, and (3) breathing, which correspond to the three states of fire, water, and wind. In the fourth absorption there is no cause [or seed] of the external destruction {of the environment} because it is not affected by the eight errors that are explained to exist in the eight abodes.
{Some say: That is through the might of those of the pristine abodes, [299] because those practicing insight cannot enter the formless realm nor proceed to other realms, and nonreturners proceed to exalted states.}
Objection: Those environments of the fourth absorption would become permanent.

Pratimokṣa vows have eight types when classified by person: (1) fully ordained monk (bhikṣu), (2) fully ordained nun (bhikṣuṇī), (3) probationary nun (śikṣamāṇā), (4) novice monk (śrāmaṇera), (5) novice nun (śrāmaṇerikā), (6) male lay practitioner (upāsaka), (7) female lay practitioner (upāsikā), (8) one-day vow-holder (upavāsastha). {A commentary on Suhṛllekha describes eight types through omitting probationary nuns and dividing one-day vows into two.}
In substance there are four types of vow not attributed to a specific being: (1) the substance of the vows of fully ordained monk and nun that are similar, (2) the substance of the vows of the three — the probationary nun, as well as novice monks and novice nuns — that are similar, and (3) the substance of male and female lay vows that are similar {since they possess, respectively, the characteristics of abandoning five, eight, and ten actions to be abandoned and so on}. Here “substance” is asserted to refer to characteristics. {The first five vows are posited from the perspective of abandoning negative action and impure conduct for as long as one lives. Lay vows are posited from the perspective of abandoning negative action and sexual misbehavior for as long as one lives. One-day vows are posited from the perspective of abandoning negative action and perverse desire for just one day.} (4) One-day vows are not classified in two because they are of brief duration. {The Compendium of Ascertainment explains that [one-day vows] are also generated in eunuchs.}
Objection: How can the substance of the vows of a fully ordained monk and fully ordained nun be similar, since fully ordained monks do not possess the eight heavy rules of a bhikṣunī? Further, a probationary nun abandons twelve actions {for two years, having already abandoned the ten actions of a novice monk or novice nun}. There are six actions: (1) initiating contact with a novice monk, (2) abiding together {in an isolated, concealed place}, (3) traveling on a path with him, (4) swimming, (5) match-making, and (6) concealing the faults of a female companion. There are six associated actions: (1) touching gold, (2) shaving pubic hair, (3) eating what is not given, (4) eating stored food, (5) cutting plant roots, and (6) digging the ground. Since these are applied to probationary nuns, there is a difference in the number of actions to be abandoned.

One becomes a layperson (upāsaka) {by initially taking refuge, desiring liberation, and} through receiving the five vows abandoning (1) taking life, (2) taking what is not given, (3) engaging in sexual misconduct, (4) lying, and (5) drinking intoxicants, for the purpose of abiding as a layperson. So too, this also applies to one-day vow-holders. One becomes a one-day vow-holder (upavāsastha) through receiving the eight vows abandoning (1) taking life, (2) taking what is not given, (3) engaging in impure conduct, (4) lying, (5) drinking intoxicants, (6) applying fragrance, flower garlands, and perfume, and dancing, singing, and playing music, {which are combined and included as one}, (7) using a bed or throne that is high and large, {which are included as one}, and (8) taking food at the wrong time, for the purpose of abiding as a one-day vow-holder. One becomes a novice through receiving vows that abandon ten actions to be abandoned. From among [the previous eight, the sixth] is divided in two — singing and playing music are taken as one, and applying fragrance, flower garlands, and perfume are taken as one; and one is added — touching gold or silver. {Earlier, dancing and so on and garlands and so forth were placed in one category so that laypeople who thought that the training was excessive might abandon discouragement and for ease of teaching. The Compendium of Ascertainment mentions:
Since it is a great fault for the ordained to dance and so on, or to live by hoarding provisions, these two faults and abandoning gold and silver were taught.}
One becomes fully ordained (bhikṣu) through receiving vows abandoning all {seven actions of body and speech, or} 253 vows: the four defeats, the thirteen remainders, the thirty abandoning downfalls, the ninety {solitary} downfalls, the four individual confessions, and the 112 training faults.

yang so so thar pa'i sdom pa thob pa'i skad cig ma dang po'i rnam par rig byed dang rnam par rig byed min pa ni so so thar pa dang sdom pa {gzhi mthun pa } dang bya ba'i las kyi lam { dngos} zhes kyang bya ste/ sdig pa las{ dang por} thar pa'i phyir dang*/ lus ngag sdom pa'i phyir dang*/ las 'byung ba'i sgo'i phyir ro/ /
skad cig ma gnyis pa phan chad ni so sor thar pa'i sdom pa kho na yin gyi/ so so thar pa ni ma yin te/ so sor thar pa'i don ni pra ti mok+Sha zhes bya ba dang por thar pa yin na thar zin pa'i phyir ro/ /
las kyi lam gyi mjug yin gyi dngos ni ma yin no/{ 'di ni yang dag par blangs pa'i tshul khrims te/ bsam gtan zag med ni chos nyid kyis thob pas so/ /}

yang so so thar pa'i sdom pa dang ldan pa'i gang zag ni brgyad de dge slong la sogs pa'o/
{ phyi rol pa rnams la so thar med dam yod ce na/
de dag la yang mi dge ba sdom pa tsam yod mod kyi so so thar pa'i sdom pa ni med de/ de dag ni log lta la sogs pas nyams pa'i phyir dang*/ sred pa'i khyad par kho na la thar par 'du shes pas srid pa la brten pa'i phyir gtan du sdig pa las so so thar par byed pa ma yin no/}

Again, eight persons possess pratimokṣa vows, such as fully ordained monks and so on.
{Question: Do non-Buddhists have pratimokṣa or not?
Reply: Indeed, they possess merely the restraint of nonvirtue but not pratimokṣa vows because they have degenerated through wrong view and so on, and they rely on cyclic existence since they consider mere craving to be liberation. Therefore they do not continuously seek liberation from immorality.}

bsam gtan gyi sdom pa dang ldan pa ni ji srid ma btang gi bar du rtag tu 'das pa dang ma 'ongs pa'i rnam par rig byed ma yin pa gtong rgyus bar du ma chod pa'i thob pa dag dang yang ldan no zhes bya ba ni grub pa'i mtha'o/{ de yang skad cig ma dang po la yang tshe gzhan dang 'dir btang ba'i bsam gtan gyi sdom pa 'das pa thal lo/ /
'on kyang nges 'byed cha mthun gyis bsdus pa na ma gtogs so zhes 'chad do/ /}
'phags pa zag med kyi sdom pa ni skad cig ma dang po sdug bsngal la chos shes pa'i bzod pa'i tshe ni 'das pa dang ldan pa min te/ zag med kyi lam sngon ma bskyed pas de lta bu'i 'das pa mi srid pa'i phyir ro/ {gal te 'bras bu thob pa dang dbang po 'pho ba'i dus na'ang 'di mtshungs pa min nam zhe na
dbyig bshes na re de la zag med kyi sdom pa'i 'dra ba yod pas de ni mi brjod do zhes zer ro/ TIka byed 'dra ba'i don zag bcas kyi sdom pa yin na skad cig dang po'i tshe'ang yod la/ zag med yin na de btang nas ji ltar yod/ des na sngon ma bskyed pas/ de dag gi dang po'ang 'das pa dang mi ldan no zhes 'dod do }

It is a philosophic tenet that those who possess absorption vows (dhyānasaṃvara) continuously possess the obtainment of past and future nonindicative form for as long as they are not relinquished or interrupted by the cause of relinquishment. {But it would follow that in the first moment of obtainment, absorption vows relinquished in this or other lives would be past vows. However, this is explained for states except those subsumed by factors conducive to penetrative insight.}
Āryas do not possess past uncontaminated vows in the first moment of perseverance for Dharma knowledge of the truth of suffering. Since they did not previously generate the uncontaminated path, such a past state is impossible.
{Question: Isn’t this similar to when āryas obtain their result or upgrade their intellectual faculty?
Vasumitra says: Those events are similar to uncontaminated vows and therefore not mentioned here.[1051]
The Subcommentary states:
If “similar” refers to contaminated vows, then they would exist in the first moment. If it refers to uncontaminated vows, then how could they exist, having been relinquished? Therefore, since they are not previously generated, their first moment does not possess past vows.[1052]}

[Third possibility:] One possessing both is one who possesses vows, negative commitments, {or interim precepts} who is {engaged in producing virtuous or nonvirtuous} indicative form {through strong intention}.
[Fourth possibility:] One possessing neither is an ordinary being of the formless realm {for whom indicative form did not arise, though death occurred while they were not yet free of attachment.}
Similarly, one should understand the four possibilities for those possessing vows and those not possessing interim precepts and so on.

Also, just looking logically, the vows abandon seven actions, but if one relinquishes only the vow abandoning taking life, that does not relinquish the other vows because it is not a factor opposing them. {The Commentary on the Chapters on Vinaya notes:
Master Kalyāṇamitra proclaims, “Though one type of vow is broken, one is not separated from the remaining full-ordination vows because it is illogical that one would completely relinquish all vows when only one type of vow is broken. Just as it is not said that taking one type of vow is taking all types, similarly it is not said that all vows are broken by breaking one type of vow.[1101]}
[Some say:] If that were so, it would follow that by taking the vow abandoning taking life, one would also be taking the other vows. Relinquishing all types by relinquishing one type is illogical because it would follow that if the hand is severed, [336] the entire body, including the feet, would also be severed. Is this what is intended?

bsam gtan du gtogs pa'i dge ba{ gzugs can yin pa} thams cad ni 'og sa nas gong sar skyes pa dang gong sa nas 'og sar skyes pa na rim pa ltar 'og sa dang gong sas bsdus pa gtong bas na sa 'phos pa dang*/ snyoms par 'jug pa de thob nas yang nyams pa dag gis kyang gtong ba'i phyir rgyu gnyis kyis gtong ngo*/ /
dngos gzhi ma thob pa'i nyer bsdogs tsam{ dang de la brten pa'i sbyor lam la sogs pa} ni shi bas kyang gtong ste/ 'grel pa las/ ris mthun pa btang ba las kyang cung zad gtong bar 'gyur ro zhes gsungs pa'i phyir dang*/ 'og nas kyang 'phags min shi bas so zhes 'chad par 'gyur ba'i phyir ro/{ bsam gtan dngos gzhi la brten nas skyes pa'i sbyor lam na sha 'phos pas mi gtong ste/ tshe de la bden pa mthong ba'i phyir ro/ /
dngos gzhi dag pa'i cha mthun ni sa de la 'dod chags dang bral bas kyang gtong ste/ nyon mongs pa bzhin du spangs pa'i phyir zhes 'chad do/ /}

Virtuous nonphysical minds and mental factors belonging to the desire realm that obtain virtue and possess [virtuous] characteristics are relinquished through severing the root (mūlaccheda) of virtue or taking birth in the form and formless realms that are higher than the desire realm. {Since they are free of attachment, they relinquish [virtuous states] to some extent, such as relinquishing the faculty of mental dissatisfaction that is virtuous. This is explained in the commentary.[1116]
Question: Is that which is relinquished [also] abandoned?
Reply: Since contaminated virtue is abandoned by one who is free of longing desire, there are four possibilities.}

'dod pa la sogs pa'i sa{ bar ma bdun po} gang las 'dod chags dang bral ba'i 'phags pa'i gang zag{ brtan pa} yin la de yang yongs su mi nyams pa'i chos can zhig yin na ni skyes nas myong 'gyur du ma zad skye ba yang phyi la sogs pa lan grangs gzhan du'ang myong bar 'gyur ba'i las kyang mi byed de/ de ni sa de dag tu phyir mi 'ong ba'i phyir ro/ /
mthong chos la myong ba dang myong bar ma nges pa ni byed do/ /
byis pa dang 'phags pa sa de dag las 'dod chags dang bral yang yongs su nyams pa'i chos can yin na ni skyes nas myong 'gyur dang lan grangs gzhan la myong 'gyur gyi las kyang byed do/{ de'ang srid rtse'i sems thob pa'i 'phags pa'ang nyams nas gzugs su skye ba bshad pas so/ /}

The words “It arises from the maturation of karma” (4:58b) refer to that arisen from karma, such as disturbing the minds of others by the preparation of substances, and secret mantras, or causing others to take poisons or intoxicants such as beer when they do not desire to, or causing fear in other sentient beings, such as deer and so on, or setting fire to forests and driving living beings into an abyss and so on. These cannot be healed by medicine, advice, or powerful beings.
Since fear refers to the alarm in the minds of beings due to human and nonhuman spirits revealing repulsive forms and so on, it is healed by the advice of holy beings. Since harm by nonhumans is perpetrated by spirits, it is pacified by the blessings of mantra lineage holders and so on. “Unbalanced elements” (4:58c) are created by illness, and that is healed by the science of healing. Grieving beings such as the son of the sage and the brahman woman of the same clan eliminated their grief and were healed by proper conduct. {The means of healing include respectively accumulating merit and purifying misdeeds, the advice of holy beings, the blessings of mantra lineage holders and so on, the science of healing, and the means of eliminating grief as well as proper conduct.}
Objection: If disturbed minds exist for mental consciousness, and they arise from the maturation of karma, then mental feeling would be the karmic maturation of nonvirtuous karma.

Vaibhāṣikas say: In that case then even the nonindicative form that exists at that time cannot be the actual karmic path because it cannot kill after death. [363] Therefore, though it cannot be the karmic path at that time, just as nonindicative form can be an actual karmic path, indicative form can be an actual karmic path, for it is similar. Therefore, if the actual karmic path refers to those who have already died, then the term “preparation” would be applied to the actual karmic path. If the actual karmic path refers to death, then the term “preparation” would be applied to the conclusion.
Again, the karmic path constituting the preparation for another act and the conclusion constituting the actual path for another act exist, because some, after killing, plunder others’ wealth, sleep with their wives, utter lies, and so on.
{Vasubandhu asserts that the three types of mental karma are not preparation because they do not initiate such action.}

All past, future, and present results that are generated by past contaminated phenomena comprise the four results, {excluding the result of separation since it is not subsumed by time.}
Question: What are they?
Reply: They are those except for results of separation. The result of separation is impossible because it does not belong to the [three] realms and it is not subsumed by time.

