dbyen gzhan ni mtshams med ma yino*/ /

rigs pas rab tu bcug pa'i las ni slob pa rigs pa thabs kyis rab tu bcug pa'i lus dang ngag dang yid kyi las rnam pa gsum kun nas slong ba mtshungs ldan dang bcas pa ni phung po lnga'i rang bzhin yin no/ /
de la ka la sogs pa'i yig 'bru'i gzugs bri ba dang*/ lha la sogs pa'i 'bur gyi rgya rko ba ni lus kyi las dang*/ dngos po gcig la sogs pa bgrang ba dang bcas pa'i snyan ngag ni ngag gi las so/{ kun slong dang bcas pa/ /}
bcu pa bcu la brgya'o zhes bya ba la sogs pa'i grangs bsdoms te 'dzin pa ni yid kyi las te {phung po bzhi'o } de ltar na go rim bzhin du shes par bya'o/ /

1. The process of the continuous obtainment of negative tendencies that stabilize their root {uninterruptedly}
2. The process of the continuous [generation of] negative tendencies that maintains their continuity {by frequent and uninterrupted generation}
3. The process of the field, one’s mindstream, which acts as the basis of negative tendencies {since it produces the harvest of the body}
4. The process of establishing natural outflow and generating branch afflictions
5. The process of establishing karmic existence
6. The process of grasping one’s [perishable] composite that is inappropriate attention
7. The process of nescience [or ignorance] that is confused about focal objects
8. The process of directing the continuum of consciousness that is projected as existence
9. The process of degenerating through moving from virtue
10. The process of not transcending the realms to which one is bound {since affliction belonging to the realms obstructs transcendence}
The ten aspects are asserted to be properly taught in this context.

{'dir nga rgyal bdun gcig tu bsdus pa ni sems khengs nas yul la rtogs par mtshungs pas gcig tu bzhag la/ lta ba la rtog tshul mi 'dra ba lnga yod pas lngar dbye ba'o/ /
yang kha cig nga'o snyam pa dang log pa'i nga rgyal ni mthong spang yin la/ gzhan lnga sgom spang du 'dod pa yod pas
de bsal ba'i don du }

{Here condensing the seven types of pride into one type is to posit them as one, since they are similar in how they conceive their object through inflating the mind. However, view is classified in five, since there are five different ways that view conceives its object.
Again, some say: Pride thinking “I” and wrong pride are objects to be abandoned by the path of seeing. The other five are objects abandoned by the path of meditation.
Therefore, to clarify that meaning:}

Objects to be abandoned by the path of meditation are killing (vadha) and so on, which includes any nonvirtuous physical and verbal karma, {namely, the seven negative actions of body and speech,} such as taking that which is not given. They are also those seven actions motivated by the afflicted entanglements (paryavasthāna) because they focus on material objects that are abandoned by the path of meditation exclusively. {Though such actions have not been abandoned, they are not practiced, because the wrong view that enhances them has been abandoned, just like one who breaks his leg does not get up.}
Objection: Because the afflicted view of the self and so on also focuses on the contaminated appropriated aggregates that are objects to be abandoned by the path of meditation, [afflicted view of the self] would become an object to be abandoned by the path of meditation.
Reply: Those that focus on objects abandoned by the path of meditation alone are objects abandoned by the path of meditation. Indeed, the afflicted view of the self and so on does focus on objects abandoned by the path of meditation, but since it does not focus on them exclusively, they are not objects to be abandoned by the path of meditation.[1422] This is explained by the Subcommentary.

'dir yul chos thams cad ni rnam pa bcu drug tu 'dus te/ khams gsum na spang bya ris lnga lnga dang*/ zag pa med pa'o/{ zag med ni 'dus ma byas kyang yin no/ /}
yul can sems thams cad kyang rnam pa bcu drug po de dag tu 'dus so/ /
de'i phyir chos du zhig sems gang gi yul du 'gyur ba shes na dmigs pa'i sgo nas rgyas pa shes par 'gyur ba'i phyir de dpyad do/{ de yang mthong spang ni phung po bzhi dang sgom spang ni phung po lngas bsdus so/ /}

Reply: Obscurations are explained as just five types because they serially harm the three aggregations (skandha) of ethics, wisdom, and concentration. Longing for desire-realm objects and malice are not free of desire or immorality; lethargy-sleep and excitation-regret delay insight and calm abiding respectively; and without concentration and wisdom, one would doubt the aggregation of concentration and wisdom or the four truths. Other obscurations are not like this. This is asserted by most Vaibhāṣikas.
Some assert: If that is so, then the sūtras should state excitation-regret prior to lethargy-sleep. But since that is not so, lethargy-sleep and excitation-regret must respectively harm the aggregation of concentration and wisdom because those striving for concentration and wisdom fear those two obscurations and are deceived by states similar to them. Similarly, doubt harms wisdom because, having arisen, it obstructs the decisive conception of phenomena.[1508] Other assertions are like the former.
Other past masters assert that five are taught because when meditators queue for alms, attachment or aversion can arise upon seeing the appealing or unappealing aspects of the substances. Therefore, later, first longing for desire-realm objects and malice obstruct preparation to enter concentration; and having entered concentration, lethargy-sleep obstructs the actual basis of calm abiding; and after that, excitation-regret and doubt obstruct insight; and after arising from concentration, doubt obstructs the decisive conception of phenomena.[1509] {Just as there is no chance of seeing a reflection when water is disturbed by the wind, one has no opportunity to see [the four truths] [484] when the mind is disturbed by these obscurations.}

{'di ni 'dod pa la bsams kyi gong ma'i de dag ni dmigs pa shes pas spong ba'o zhes so }
gal te dmigs pa yin kyang spong bar ma nges so zhe na/
yul can skal ba mnyam pa'i khams kun 'gro snga mas bskyed pa'i skal ba mi mnyam pa'i khams kun 'gro rnams dmigs pa yin pa na/ sdug bsngal dang kun 'byung la chos bzod chos shes kyi rgyu 'dod pa'i skal ba mnyam pa'i khams kun 'gro rnams spangs pas de dag gis skyed pa'i 'bras bu skal ba mi mnyam pa'i khams kun 'gro rnams kyang spong ste/ rgyu med pa'i 'bras bu mi srid pa'i phyir ro/ /

{They contemplate the desire realm but are abandoned by knowledge focusing on those of higher realms.}
Objection: They are focal objects, but they are not to be abandoned.
Reply: Minds that are pervasive states of the same realm focus on pervasive states of different realms generated earlier. But since Dharma perseverance and Dharma knowledge of the truths of suffering and origin abandon causal pervasive states [focusing on] the same desire realm, they also abandon pervasive states [focusing on] different realms that are results generated by them, because a result without a cause is impossible. [485]

sa re re'i nyon mongs pa'i rnam pa dgu dgu byas te chen po'i chen po la sogs pa gang gi gnyen po lam chung ngu'i chung ngu la sogs pa gang yin pa de skyes pas nyon mongs pa de zad cing spangs par 'gyur gyi mthong lam gyis dmigs pa bden bzhi shes kyang sgom spang mi spong {ste bden pa rnams sngar shes par byas zin } bas dmigs pa shes pas kyang ma yin zhing*/ dang po'i rnam pa spangs kyang de'i/ /
yul can dri ma lhag rnams kyis/ /
zhes pas yul can kun 'gro dang sgom spang dang po brgyad kyi bar du spangs kyang yul sgom spang phyi ma mi spong bas dmigs byed zad pas kyang ma yin la/ dmigs yul sgom spang snga ma brgyad spangs kyang yul can phyi ma dgu pa mi spong bas dmigs yul spangs pas kyang ma yin no/{ de ltar na sgom spang ni rang rang gi gnyen po skyes pas spong ba dang*/ rang rgyud kyi sgom spang nyon mongs can gzugs can dang nyon mongs can ma yin pa dang*/ gzhan rgyud kyi thams cad ni yul can zad pas spong bas sgom spang spong tshul ni rnam pa gnyis yin no/}

Reply: It is asserted that afflictions are abandoned through counteracting focal objects but not through {the antidote and that which is abandoned} being concomitant. {If they were concomitant,} their union could not be undone, since {these two} would arise inseparably, but they can be separated due to their [different] focal objects.
Question: To what extent can it be said that afflictions are abandoned?
Reply: {Afflictions are abandoned at the time of the path due to present negative tendencies ceasing to exist in one’s mindstream, and the path that achieves cessation abandons afflictions through taking past and future afflictions as focal objects. Further, afflictions are abandoned through understanding the mode of being of objects. But if one does not understand the mode of being of a specific object, then one will obtain in one’s mindstream afflictions that do not possess that as their object, by the power of the afflictions that do possess that as their object. Thus it is said, “Without counteracting the object, one cannot abandon awareness of that object.”} Further, afflicted states that exist in one’s mindstream are abandoned through stopping their obtainment and generating the obtainment of separation from them. Abandoning all unafflicted states, contaminated virtue, and unobscured-neutral physical and nonphysical states within one’s mindstream — and also abandoning the afflicted longing desire within one’s mindstream that is a mind focusing on them — is designated as abandoning them. And the scripture revealing this has already been explained. {Further, objects are simultaneously abandoned by the path of seeing and serially abandoned by the path of meditation. But it is said that if childlike beings were to abandon objects to be abandoned by the path of seeing, they would do so serially.}

They are distant through incompatible characteristics,
opposing factors, separate places, and time;
for example, the primary elements, ethics,
sites, and the two times. (5:62)

bsgom pa'i lam ni zag bcas zag med rnam pa gnyis yin te/ 'jig rten pa'i sgom lam zag bcas kyi bsam gtan gzugs med la zag pa rgyas par 'gyur ba'i phyir dang*/ 'jig rten las 'das pa'i sgom lam bden pa'i rnam pa can bden pa mthong don goms par byed pa la zag pa rgyas pa med pa'i phyir ro/ /
mthong ba'i lam zhes bya ba ni gcig tu zag pa med pa yin te/ khams gsum pa'i{ mthong spang} gnyen po yin pa'i phyir dang*/ mthong spang chen po'i chen po la sogs pa rnams dgu cig car spong ba'i phyir dang*/{ de zag med yin pas khyab ste} 'jig rten pa la ni de 'dra ba'i mthu med pa'i phyir ro/ {sgom spang ni bstan pas gzhi dang bcas pa'i phyir cig car mi spong ngo*/ /
mthong sgom gnyis khyad par ni mang ste/ ngo bo las ni zag med kho na dang*/ zag bcas zag med gnyis ka dang*/ dmigs pa las bden bzhi dang gong sa 'og sa gnyis ka la dmigs pa dang*/ rnam pa las bden pa dang zhi rags kyang yin pa dang*/ ngo bo'i dbye ba las bzod shes gnyis dang shes pa kho na yin pa dang*/ spang bya spong tshul las dmigs pa shes pas cig car spong ba dang*/ gnyen po goms pas rim gyis spong ba dang*/ rten las 'dod pa dang khams gsum ga dang*/ rgyud las 'phags pa kho na dang*/ skye 'phags gnyis ka dang*/ skye tshul las stan gcig las skye ba dang skye ba gzhan la'ang skye ba'o }

Thus one cultivates the close placement of mindfulness focusing on uncombined states such as the body alone. Having gained familiarity with that, then close placement of mindfulness of phenomena (dharmasmṛtyupasthāna) places the mind on general focal objects (ālambana) through combining the body, feeling, mind, and phenomena, and it views and analyzes those general focal objects in terms of their aspects: impermanent (anitya), suffering (duḥkha), empty (śūnya), and selfless (anātma).
{The three that focus on specific characteristics, general characteristics, and their combined state are said to correspond to the three stages of the path of accumulation: the small, medium, and great.}
This is taught in this context because both calm abiding and insight are the main practice of the path of accumulation. Further, all individual virtue of the awakening mind that exists prior to the path of preparation is the path of accumulation.

Therefore directing the mind to the aspects of the four noble truths is the initial meditation. Enhancement is cultivating and purifying that meditation. The medium stage of patience, for example, meditates on its focal objects and aspects through steadily reducing their number.
The pristine wisdom of patience (kṣānti) arises from enhancing, respectively, the small, medium, and great stages of the peak. During heat and peak, there is, respectively, the small and medium engagement of the noble truths. As the realization [achieved] on the peak [level] is progressively cultivated during the patience level, there is great engagement of the noble truths, since [this level] does not degenerate.
Patience also has three stages. Both small and medium patience first target and establish the noble truths through the close placement of mindfulness of phenomena, just as first heat and peak target and establish the aspects of the noble truths in accordance with the close placement of mindfulness of phenomena that targets and establishes those aspects. {Therefore [the peak stage and so on] arise with the essential nature of the close placement of mindfulness of phenomena.}

de ltar na 'dir bzod pa chen po dang chos mchog{ dang sdug bsngal la chos bzod dang} gsum 'dod pa'i sdug bsngal gyi bden pa kho na la dmigs shing skad cig re re yin par bstan pas dro bar gyur pas na bzod pa 'bring phal che ba'i bar ni khams gsum gyi bden bzhi la ci rigs par dmigs shing rgyun chags pa yin par shes par bya'o/{ gzhan yang bzod pa chen po dang*/ chos mchog skad cig ma re rer bstan pas dro bar gyur pa nas bzod pa 'bring gi bar la rnam pa gtod 'phel gyi khyad par yod gyi bzod pa chen po dang*/ chos mchog gnyis la ni rnam pa gtod 'phel gyi khyad par med par mngon te/ 'grel pa las/ rnam pa gtod pa ni dang po nyid du bden pa rnams la rnam pa dag 'god pa'o/ /
zhes pa dang*/ TIka las 'phel bar byed pa ni goms pas byang bar byed pa'o/ /
zhes dang po sgom pa dang de'i rgyun goms par byed pa la brjod pa na skad cig ma la de dag mi rung ba'i phyir ro/ /}

Vaibhāṣikas assert that all four factors conducive to penetrative insight depend on the body of the desire realm; there are none that depend on a body of the higher realms. Further, the first three are initially generated among humans of the three continents, and those who have already generated them manifest them among the gods. {Great patience} and the supreme mundane state can also be first generated among the gods. The first three are obtained by the basis of both, since males obtain the basis of a female and females also obtain the basis of a male. {I think this refers to periods when there was mutual change of sex and birth in atypical states.}
The supreme mundane state is obtained by females in dependence on either a male or female physical basis, but males do not obtain it in dependence on both bases. A male who obtains the supreme mundane state is not born as a female since he obtains the nonanalytical cessation [522] of being born female.
{One tenet system asserts that immediately after the supreme mundane state, the path of seeing is generated for males but it is not generated for females.}
The factors conducive to penetrative insight are contaminated virtuous states practiced in the form realm, and their karmic maturation is the five aggregates of a being of the form realm. They act as antidotes to cyclic existence since they counteract existence through ascertaining the aspects of impermanence and so on. Therefore they act as completing karma and do not act as projecting karma of states of similar type. However, absorption associated with degeneration acts as projecting karma. This is explained by Master Pūrṇavardhana.[1595]
Question: How are factors conducive to penetrative insight relinquished?

Those who generate the factors conducive to penetrative insight in dependence on the actual basis (maula) of absorption see the truths in that life because they have strong aversion to saṃsāra. {It is said that it is uncertain that those possessing the path of preparation dependent on unrestricted access concentration and special absorption see the four truths in this life, since those states can degenerate. But it is definite that all paths of preparation dependent on the actual bases are relinquished by āryas.}
If {medium patience and below} were initially obtained and then degenerate but are again obtained {later, only} states that were not previously existent [523] are newly obtained. Past states are not reobtained in the way pratimokṣa vows are reobtained. Past states are not reobtained, {because} they are not cultivated frequently {since preparation for the ārya path is not generated}, and {they are not obtained through freedom from attachment}. Also {they were not previously obtained on one’s level, since} they were not established through exertion. {If there is one who restores them due to a single knowledge through prayer, and, if they do not arise from proliferation, they are generated from the basis.}
Question: What is the difference between broad degeneration and complete degeneration?

Those abiders in the result of once return who exhaust (kṣīṇa) seven or eight stages of fault (doṣa) of desire-realm objects to be abandoned by the path of meditation are called “special abiders.” They are once returners with one life (ekajanma) {until they obtain the state of an arhat,} or else they are once returners with one obstruction (ekavīcika), {or affliction, to obtaining nonreturn}.
Once returners obstructed by one life to attaining nonreturn, as explained before, necessarily transcend the desire realm. Regarding transcending the realm, the Abhidharmakośa notes, “Obtaining patience, nonreturn, and arhat, absolutely obstruct karma” (4:107cd). [541] They are also once returners obstructed by one affliction because affliction is like karma and difficult to abandon.
{Prior to obtaining the path of seeing, those who obtain the ordinary result where three or four stages are abandoned, or seven or eight stages are abandoned, are not stream enterers born the same type or once returners with one obstruction, because they have not obtained the uncontaminated faculty that is their antidote. When they manifest the special result of them or their higher antidote, they become those.}

dper na khyed rang ltar na 'jig rten las 'das pa'i lam gyis srid pa'i rtse mo'i phyed ni mtshon pa ste nyon mongs pa brgyad pa man chad ci rigs pa las rnam par grol ba'i 'phags pa phyir mi 'ong ba dbang po rtul po bsam gtan la brten nas dbang po 'pho ba la srid rtse'i gnyen po 'jig rten pa'i lam med pa'i phyir 'jig rten pa'i bral thob kyang med la/ 'jig rten las 'das pa'i bral thob kyang bsam gtan la brten nas dbang po 'pho bas btang bas bral thob gnyis ka med kyang srid rtse'i nyon mongs mi ldan par 'dod pa bzhin dang*/ yang dper na bsam gtan dang po las gong du bsam gtan gnyis pa la sogs par skyes pa'i byis pa rnams la'ang bsam gtan gtogs pa'i dge ba ni/ sa 'phos nyams pa dag gis gtong zhes pas 'dod pa'i nyon mongs spangs pa'i bral thob gnyis ka{ skye ba'i sa 'phos pas btang na} med kyang 'dod pa'i nyon mongs mi ldan pa bzhin{ 'dir yang gong ma'i nyon mongs dang mi ldan} te 'di ni gnod pa'i dpe gnyis pa yin no/ /
'dir yang 'phags pa 'jig rten las 'das pa'i lam gyis gzugs med las 'dod chags dang bral ba bsam gtan la brten nas dbang po 'pho ba dag la gong ma'i nyon mongs mi ldan pa ni sa bon spangs pa'i phyir yin la/ bsam gtan dang po la sogs par skyes pa la 'dod pa la sogs pa 'og ma'i nyon mongs mi ldan pa ni sa bon yod kyang mngon du 'gyur ba'i rkyen ma tshang bas yin gyi bral ba'i thob pa yod pas ni ma yin no zhes mdo sde pa la sogs pa 'dod do/{ 'phags pa ni sa 'phos pas bral thob zag bcas gtong yang*/ zag med na 'bras bu thob sbyangs nyams pa las zhes pa'i rgyu med par mi gtong ngo*/ /}
'o na sa gang gis sa gang las 'dod chags dang 'bral zhe na/

for there are two examples that harm your assertion. (1) The first example is that, according to you, ārya nonreturners of dull faculty who are liberated from up to eight afflictions, or half the summit of existence, [553] who upgrade their intellectual faculty dependent on the absorptions do not obtain mundane freedom because there is no mundane path that is an antidote to the afflictions of the summit of existence. Though they obtain transcendent freedom, they relinquish that through upgrading their intellectual faculty dependent on the absorptions. But they do not possess afflictions of the summit of existence even though they obtain neither freedom. (2) The second example is childlike beings, who are born in absorptions higher than the first absorption but do not possess desire-realm affliction. {Nor do they possess higher affliction,} even though they have neither mundane nor transcendent obtainment of freedom that abandons desire affliction {if that is relinquished by relocating from the level of birth}. As the Abhidharmakośa notes, “Virtue belonging to the absorptions is relinquished through changing level or degeneration” (4:40ab).
Sautrāntikas and so on assert that in this context, āryas who are free from attachment to formless affliction through the transcendent path, and who upgrade their intellectual faculty dependent on the absorptions, do not possess afflictions of lower levels, such as the desire realm, because they abandon their seeds. Those who are born in the first absorption and so on and do not possess the afflictions of lower levels such as the desire realm do not obtain freedom since they possess seeds of affliction even though the conditions to awaken those seeds do not assemble. {Though āryas relinquish contaminated obtainment of freedom through changing level, they do not relinquish that which is uncontaminated without a reason. As the Abhidharmakośa declares, “[The virtue of absorption of] the ārya [is relinquished] by obtaining the result, upgrading, or degeneration” (4:40d).}
Question: What level is free from attachment to what level?

'phags pa 'jig rten pa'i lam gyis thob pa'i 'bras bu bar pa gnyis ni{ chos can dge sbyong gi 'bras bu yin te/ lam gnyis kyi 'bras bu spangs pa'i thob pa rang rang gi 'bras bur bsdus pa gcig rnyed pa'i phyir dang} sgom lam zag bcas{ kyi spangs pa kho na min gyi} mthong lam zag med kyi spangs pa{ 'ang der} bsres nas thob pa'i phyir dang*/ 'bras bu bar pa gnyis kyis bsdus pa'i zag med kyi thob pa gcig 'dzin pa'i phyir dge sbyong gi tshul gyi 'bras bu yin no/ /
