Reply: They are differentiated in eight ways such as by nature and so on: {as it is said, “nature, focus, aspect, level, continuum, obtainment, protection, and similar and dissimilar.”}
Compassion (1) has the nature of nonaversion; (2) it possesses the aspect of the suffering of pain; (3) it focuses on sentient beings of the desire-realm alone; (4) it exists on four levels, including unrestricted access concentration and special absorption that are both subsumed in the first absorption, the first absorption itself, and so on; (5) it exists in the mindstreams of śrāvakas and ordinary beings; (6) it is obtained through freedom from desire-realm attachment; (7) it does not fully protect from the suffering of saṃsāra, since that practiced by śrāvakas and so on involves ordinary compassion; and (8) it is compassion applied unevenly to all sentient beings, since it is compassion generated to those experiencing the suffering of pain alone.
Great compassion has (1) the nature of nondelusion; (2) it possesses the aspect of the three sufferings; (3) it focuses on sentient beings of the three realms; (4) it exists on the level of the fourth absorption alone, because that level alone has the potency to generate it through flexibility of concentration; (5) it exists in the mindstream of a buddha; (6) it is obtained through freedom from attachment to the summit of existence; (7) it fully protects from the great fear of saṃsāra when such compassion is practiced for all sentient beings; and (8) it is compassion evenly applied to all sentient beings, whether they experience pain or not.

Absence of conflict (araṇā) refers to arhats who recognize that the sufferings of sentient beings arise from the afflictions, and who recognize that they themselves constitute a special object of benefit for others. Then, with the desire to eliminate conflict in others, they enter equipoise, reflecting, “May those who see me and focus on me not generate attachment, aversion, pride, and negligence.” {Since this path does not cause anyone to suffer, it is the absence of conflict and a special skill that is a means to protect the minds of others}. Then, having arisen from that equipoise, they generate subsequent knowledge that is a state of conventional knowledge (saṃvṛtijñāna). {This knowledge possesses as its object only the future affliction in others’ mindstreams to be abandoned by the path of meditation. Therefore there is no equivalent uncontaminated knowledge.}
Its level is the lower limit of the fourth absorption alone, since it is the supreme path among the easily established paths. The person establishing it is an immutable (akopya) arhat. It is not other arhats such as those who degenerate, because sometimes beings susceptible to degeneration cannot eliminate affliction from their mindstreams, {and that also applies to the others}. It is solely generated by the physical basis of a human (nṛjā) of the three continents because such beings have clarity of mind {and they have companions with many afflictions}.
Its focal object is others who possess attachment and aversion. And because it cultivates the thought “May affliction not arise in them,” it takes as its object field (gocara) that which is to be abandoned by the path of meditation. This includes (1) afflictions belonging to the desire realm, (2) those that are future unarisen states (anutpanna), and (3) those possessing a basis (savastu), such as attachment that is an object to be abandoned by the path of meditation. That is (1) because little affliction is observed to arise in those of upper realms and āryas, (2) because previously arisen states cannot be abandoned, and (3) because objects to be abandoned by the path of seeing, which are pervasive [negative tendencies] focusing on those considered to lack a basis [of affliction], cannot be abandoned by focusing on all [truths]. This is due to the fact that they focus on the mindstreams of others; whereas objects to be abandoned by meditation, which possess a basis [of affliction], can be abandoned by focusing on the first two truths. [626]

don rnams kyis 'gal ba dang 'brel ba don dang 'brel ba'i rig pa dang*/ gzhan la thags thogs med par mngon par brjod pa la thogs pa med par shes pa dang*/ rang nyid yid la byed pas zhi gnas lhag mthong gi ting nge 'dzin mi brjed pas lam la dbang ba nyid la thogs pa med par shes pa ste/ dang po ni 'bel gtam la mkhas pa yin la/ gnyis pa ni rang nyid yid la byed pa la mkhas pa yin no/ /
