{ slob dpon gyis/} gal te sems thams cad skad cig ma yin pas dmigs pa 'pho long med pa'i phyir rtse gcig pa ma yin nam gang gis de dag rtse gcig par byed pa'i{ khyad par byed chos} ting nge 'dzin{ don gzhan} brtags pa ni don med do zhe na ma yin te/ skad cig dang po'i sems la mi dgos kyang skad cig ma gnyis pa'i sems{ dang po dmigs pa'am} ting nge 'dzin de'i sgo nas dmigs pa las rnam par mi g.yeng bar byed pas don dang bcas pa'i phyir ro/ /
{ zhe na/ /}
'o na de lta na sems dang po ting nge 'dzin dang mtshungs ldan de la ting nge 'dzin don med par 'gyur ba'i phyir{ te de'i bya ba mi byed pa'i phyir} dang*/ skad cig dang po'i ting nge 'dzin gnyis pa'i dus na 'gags na gnyis pa la byed pa 'jug pa 'gal ba'i phyir dang*/ ma 'gags na sems snga ma dang dus mi mtshungs par 'gyur ba'i phyir dang*/ gzhan yang dran pa ma mnyam pa la sogs pa'i rgyu mtshan gang kho nas ting nge 'dzin rtse gcig tu 'dod pa'i rgyu mtshan de kho nas sems rtse gcig par byed nus na de'i rgyur ting nge 'dzin don gzhan btags pa la dgos pa med la/ mi nus na ting nge 'dzin la'ang mtshungs pa'i phyir dang*/ gzhan yang ting nge 'dzin ni sems kyi sa mang po pa yin pas sems{ thams cad} rtse gcig par thal bar 'gyur ba'i phyir ro zhe na mi 'gyur te/ ting nge 'dzin stobs che ba dang mtshungs par ldan pa'i sems rtse gcig pa yin zhing*/ stobs chung ba dang mtshungs par ldan pa'i sems ni rtse gcig pa ma yin la/ ting nge 'dzin sa mang po tsam ni dge ba kho nar ma nges pa dang stobs chung ba'i phyir ro zhes bye brag tu smra bar 'dod do/ /

Also, regarding the physical basis, those absorption and formless meditative attainments possess a basis that is a body of the same level or a body of lower level. Meditative attainment of the summit of existence manifests for one at the summit of existence, and it manifests for those down to the desire realm, {for there are nine manifesting agents}. The meditative attainment of the first absorption manifests on its own level and on a lower-level physical basis of the desire realm as well as up to the second level. {Further, for contaminated states, it is definite that the result of its level depends on its cause of lower level.}
Question: Why does lower meditative attainment not manifest on a basis born in a higher level?
Reply: There is no need, since wherever one is born, that level is best and the lower level is inferior.

Again, a sūtra mentions three concentrations:
Concentration has three types: the concentrations of emptiness, wishlessness, and signlessness.[1910]
Nirvāṇa as an object, which is an analytical cessation, is signless since it is free of {the ten} signs: (1–5) the five objects, (6) male or (7) female characteristics, and (8–10) the three characteristics of conditioned states. {Here the faculties are not called signs since the faculties do not captivate the mind like objects.}
The concentration of signlessness (animitta) possesses four common aspects, such as the pacification and cessation of all signs, since it is concentration focusing on cessation. The concentration of emptiness (śūnyatā) focuses on that which is empty or selfless, engages the selflessness (anātman) and emptiness of the truth of suffering, and therefore possesses those common aspects. {Also it is the antidote of the afflicted view of the self.}

These three concentrations are either (1) contaminated, mundane, and pure (śuddha) or (2) transcendent and stainless (amalā).
{Some say: They are subsumed by the preparatory and subsequent states of the agent conducive to penetrative insight and the ārya path.}
The first of these exists on eleven levels: (1) desire-realm level, (2–7) the six levels of absorption, and (8–11) the four formless-realm levels. The second exists on any uncontaminated level: the nine uncontaminated levels excluding the desire-realm level, the summit of existence, and the seven access concentrations to the second absorption and so on. From among both the pure and uncontaminated, these three stainless (nirmalā) concentrations are the cause of, and the gateway to, liberation. Therefore a sūtra declares that emptiness and so on, as gateways to liberation, are also called “the three doors of liberation” (vimokṣamukhatraya).

These latter three concentrations are entirely contaminated (sāsrava) because those who do not shun the uncontaminated ārya path {view it as possessing qualities}. But those who are supported by the former three concentrations, who shun the ārya path, view it as something to be discarded. Thus they are well known as “concentrations that counteract liberation.”
The basis is humans (nṛsu) {of the three continents} since it only arises among those with clarity of mind. {The human body arises as a basis of sickness, aging, and death and induces the discernment of oneself as someone who is sick and so on. But that is incompatible with engaging uncontaminated composites. For gods that is not the case because most do not obtain offensive [or corrupt] bodies.}
The person is the immutable (akopya) arhat alone; he has the might to generate it since he has sharp faculty. Arhats with dull faculty do not; they are incapable of generating it. {Learners do not shun the ārya path since they possess superior action. Arhats liberated according to circumstances do not repudiate it since they strive to protect the ārya path. It exists on the four absorption levels, on three formless-realm levels, as well as in the desire realm.}

(3) The third, the liberation of beauty, exists in the upper limit (antya) of the fourth absorption. It is essentially nonattachment (alobha) since by nature it is the antidote to attachment; it is not repulsiveness since it possesses the aspect of beauty. It is given that name since it emanates attractive forms that it views as beautiful and it manifests that concentration through the body. {The former two, plus this, make three.} If they possess a retinue, they possess by nature five aggregates, since they have form as a complement. {The liberation of beauty does not possess [pleasure] since pleasure moves [the mind], and it is impossible that pleasure supports [the liberation of beauty].
Question: Now, since there is no attachment to color on the third, how does [the liberation of beauty] exist on the fourth absorption?}
Reply: The need {for generating these [liberations]} is that disillusionment with saṃsāra arises by contemplating repulsiveness and elation arises through joy. But if attachment to beautiful emanations arises, then the first two liberations are not established, and if it does not arise, then they are established. This should be investigated.
I think that the liberation of beauty is not taught to exist in the first three absorptions because nonattachment is not established there. For when one engages beautiful objects, joy and craving for them arise, since (1) joy and pleasure exist on those levels. Or else nonattachment is not established since (2) those absorptions are imperfect.
{Due to those two reasons, yogis generate liberation and subjugate such states, [682] distancing themselves from affliction and mastering meditative attainment. Having obtained control over such states, they accomplish feats such as transforming earth into gold, blessing their faculties to abide stably for a long time according to their resolution, relinquishing or extending lifespan, entering the atoms of Meru, and so on. And owing to those two, they accomplish ārya supernatural powers such as freedom from affliction, knowledge through prayer, the four analytical knowledges, and so on.}

gzugs med pa'i mnyam gzhag dge ba dag pa pa dang zag med rnams kho na rnam thar yin gyi nyon mongs can dang mnyam par ma bzhag pa 'chi ba'i srid pa'i sems lta bu'ang ma yin no/{ 'di dge ba kho na yin pas so/ /}
kha cig gzugs med de dag na rnam smin skyes kyi sems dang sems byung rnams la bsams nas 'chi ba'i sems las gzhan pa'i mnyam par ma bzhag pa'i sems kyang yod par 'dod do/ /
phyogs snga ma ltar na ni gzugs med mnyam par ma bzhag pa ni 'chi ba'i srid pa'i gnas skabs kyi sems dang sems byung kho na yin par 'dod do/ /
gzugs med kyi dngos gzhir ma zad nyer bsdogs kyi rnam grol gyi lam gyis kyang rnam thar gyi ming thob kyi bar chad med pa'i lam gyis ni rnam thar gyi ming mi thob ste/ rnam thar ni 'og sa la rgyab kyis phyogs pa yin na de ni sa 'og ma la dmigs pa'i phyir ro/{ de yang dang po gnyis kha dog gi 'dod chags la dang*/ gsum pa ni mi sdug pa la dang*/ gzugs med ni dmigs pa 'og ma dag la rgyab kyis phyogs pa'i phyir ro/ /}
